erial sword enchantment 8, damage 1000 → payout 400 (high-enchantment clause applies, then deductible)\" - high enchantment wins\n- \"dragon material sword enchantment 5, damage 800 → payout 700 (full reimbursement, then deductible: 800 - 100)\" - dragon material clause applies\n\nFor the third test: enchantment 5, dragon material, damage 800 → 700. With my refactor:\n- isHighEnchant = (5 >= 8) = false\n- grossDamagePayout = 800 (not high enchant)\n- totalRaw += 800 - 100 = 700 ✓\n\nGood, works.\n\nWhat about a hypothetical \"dragon material staff enchantment 5, damage 500 → 400\"? Same logic, works.\n\nOK so the refactor is safe. Let me also think about whether the \"dragon material clause\" is truly just \"default behavior\". Re-reading:\n\n\"Dragon material clause (no high enchantment)\":\n- \"dragon material sword enchantment 5, damage 800 → payout 700 (full reimbursement, then deductible: 800 - 100)\"\n\nSo the dragon material clause = \"full reimbursement\" + deductible. The non-dragon default = \"full reimbursement\" + deductible. They're the same! So the dragon material \"clause\" is implicit - it's the absence of any special clause.\n\nThis makes the `isDragon` check truly redundant. We can safely remove it.\n\nActually wait - I'm wondering if the dragon material might affect the calculation differently than I think. Let me re-check by computing both:\n\nFor damage 500, sword enchantment 3 (steel, no dragon):\n- isHighEnchant = false\n- With current code: fullPay = 500 - 100 = 400. totalRaw = 400. payout = 400. ✓ matches test.\n\nFor damage 500, sword enchantment 3 (dragon material):\n- isHighEnchant = false  \n- With current code: isDragon = true, fullPay = 500 - 100 = 400. totalRaw = 400. payout = 400.\n\nSame result. The dragon material doesn't change anything in the current logic. So removing the `isDragon` ternary is safe.\n\nNow let me write the refactor and run tests to verify.\n\nActually, I realize I should also think about whether to extract a helper function. The benefit:\n- Clearer intent: `grossDamagePayout` says \"the gross amount the policy pays before deductible\"\n- Easier to read the main loop\n- Named constants for the deductible\n\nThe cost:\n- Adds a function (extra binding, extra invocation)\n- Higher APP mass\n\nBut Rule 2 (Reveals Intent) trumps APP. Let me weigh:\n\nOption A (minimal refactor, remove dead code):\n```typescript\nexport function processClaim(\n  policy: Policy,\n  incident: Incident,\n): { payout: number; remainingCap: number } {\n  let totalRaw = 0;\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {\n    const item = policy.items.find((i) => i.type === damage.itemType);\n    const isHighEnchant = (item?.enchantment ?? 0) >= 8;\n    const gross = isHighEnchant ? damage.amount * 0.5 : damage.amount;\n    totalRaw += gross - 100;\n  }\n  const payout = Math.min(Math.floor(totalRaw), policy.remainingCap);\n  return { payout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout };\n}\n```\n\nOption B (extract helper):\n```typescript\nconst DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE = 100;\n\nfunction grossDamagePayout(amount: number, item: PolicyItem | undefined): number {\n  const isHighEnchant = (item?.enchantment ?? 0) >= 8;\n  return isHighEnchant ? amount * 0.5 : amount;\n}\n\nexport function processClaim(...): ... {\n  let totalRaw = 0;\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {\n    const item = policy.items.find((i) => i.type === damage.itemType);\n    totalRaw += grossDamagePayout(damage.amount, item) - DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE;\n  }\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nOption A is more concise but mixes the clause logic with the loop. Option B separates concerns but adds a function. \n\nI think Option A is fine - the logic is now short enough to inline. Let me go with Option A and possibly add a named constant for 100:\n\nActually let me also check: is there a constant for the deductible elsewhere? No. Let me name it for clarity. But it would add a binding. Let me see if it's worth it.\n\nNaming priority: `100` in `gross - 100` is a magic