e);\n  if (!item) {\n    throw new Error(`Damage references item type not in policy: ${damage.itemType}`);\n  }\n  totalRaw += computeDamagePayout(item, damage);\n}\n```\n\nThis is cleaner. The for-loop now reads: \"for each damage, find the item and add its contribution\".\n\nLet me also think about whether to use `reduce` for the total. Currently we use a for-loop with `totalRaw` accumulator and then `Math.floor(totalRaw)`. The for-loop with mutation is a bit clunky. We could use reduce:\n\n```typescript\nconst totalRaw = incident.damages.reduce((sum, damage) => {\n  const item = policy.items.find((i) => i.type === damage.itemType);\n  if (!item) {\n    throw new Error(`Damage references item type not in policy: ${damage.itemType}`);\n  }\n  return sum + computeDamagePayout(item, damage);\n}, 0);\n```\n\nThis eliminates the mutable `totalRaw`. But it makes the loop body more complex inline. \n\nI think extracting `computeDamagePayout` is the cleanest refactor. Let me also consider: can we extract a `findOrThrow` helper to clean up the find/throw pattern?\n\n```typescript\nfunction findPolicyItem(policy: Policy, itemType: string): PolicyItem {\n  const item = policy.items.find((i) => i.type === itemType);\n  if (!item) {\n    throw new Error(`Damage references item type not in policy: ${itemType}`);\n  }\n  return item;\n}\n```\n\nThen:\n```typescript\nfor (const damage of incident.damages) {\n  const item = findPolicyItem(policy, damage.itemType);\n  totalRaw += computeDamagePayout(item, damage);\n}\n```\n\nThat's clean. The processClaim now reads very clearly.\n\nLet me also think about whether the type for `itemType: string` should be more specific. The test \"damage with unknown itemType → throws Error\" suggests we may want to enforce that the itemType is one of the known types. But this is a future test (it's `it.todo`), so we should wait for it.\n\nActually, looking at `INSURANCE_VALUES: Record<string, number>`, the keys are all known types. We could define an `ItemType` union type and use it. But that might be premature - the tests don't require this yet.\n\nLet me make my refactoring focused on:\n1. Extract `computeDamagePayout(item, damage)` - this reveals the high-enchantment rule clearly\n2. Extract `findPolicyItem(policy, itemType)` - this names the lookup-and-throw pattern\n\nActually wait, looking again. `findPolicyItem` returning the item feels less clean than just inline. The find/throw is only used in one place. Inlining is fine.\n\nLet me just extract `computeDamagePayout`. That's the single most valuable refactoring.\n\nActually I realize I should consider whether the existing code is clear enough. Let me read it again:\n\n```typescript\nlet totalRaw = 0;\nfor (const damage of incident.damages) {\n  const item = policy.items.find((i) => i.type === damage.itemType);\n  if (!item) {\n    throw new Error(`Damage references item type not in policy: ${damage.itemType}`);\n  }\n  const isHighEnchant = (item.enchantment ?? 0) >= 8;\n  const gross = isHighEnchant ? damage.amount * 0.5 : damage.amount;\n  totalRaw += gross - DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE;\n}\n```\n\nThis is actually pretty readable. The variable names are clear:\n- `totalRaw` - total raw payout before cap/flooring\n- `isHighEnchant` - is high enchantment?\n- `gross` - gross amount (before deductible)\n\nThe high-enchantment rule is \"clear enough\" in context but extracting it makes the algorithm explicit.\n\nOK, let me commit to this refactoring plan:\n\n1. Extract `computeDamagePayout(item: PolicyItem, damage: Damage): number` - encapsulates the high-enchantment rule and deductible subtraction\n2. Optionally extract a constant `HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_MULTIPLIER = 0.5` for the 50% rule\n\nHmm, but extracting the multiplier just to avoid `* 0.5` is not really a win. The 0.5 is self-explanatory.\n\nLet me also check if the lookup is inefficient. For each damage, we do a linear find through policy.items. This is O(n*m) where n=damages, m=items. For a more efficient version, we could build a Map upfront. But for the typical small number of items (test cases have 1-4 items), this is fine.\n\nLet me focus on the simplest, clearest refactoring: extract `computeDamagePayout`.\n\nActually, I think I should do TWO things to make the refactoring substantive:\n\n1. Extract `computeDamagePayout(item, damage)` - this clarifies per-damage logic\n2. Refactor `processClaim` to use `reduce` instead of a for-loop with mutable accumulator, OR keep the for-loop for clarity\n\nLet me check the for-loop vs reduce debate. The for-loop with `let totalRaw = 0` and `totalRaw += ...` is a common JavaScript pattern. The reduce version eliminates the mutation but is more functional. Both are fine.\n\nFor Rule 4 (Fewest Elements), the for-loop has 2 assignments (init + update). Reduce has 1 invocation + 1 closure (more elements). For-loop is fewer elements.\n\nOK, sticking with for-loop, just extracting `computeDamagePayout`.\n\nLet me also think about whether to introduce an early-return for empty damages:\n```typescript\nif (incident.damages.length === 0) {\n  return { payout: 0, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap };\n}\n```\n\nThis would short-circuit but doesn't add much value. The for-loop handles empty arrays correctly (totalRaw stays 0).\n\nOK let me just do the extraction. Here's my plan:\n\n```typescript\nconst DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE = 100;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD = 8;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_FACTOR = 0.5;\n\nconst INSURANCE_VALUES: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 1000,\n  amulet: 600,\n  staff: 800,\n  potion: 400,\n  rune: 250,\n  moonstone: 250,\n};\n\nexport type PolicyItem = {\n  type: string;\n  material?: string;\n  enchantment?: number;\n  cursed?: boolean;\n};\n\nexport type Policy = {\n  items: PolicyItem[];\n  insuranceSum: number;\n  cap: number;\n  remainingCap: number;\n};\n\nexport type Damage = { itemType: string; amount: number };\n\nexport type Incident = { cause: string; damages: Damage[] };\n\nexport function createPolicy(items: PolicyItem[]): Policy {\n  const insuranceSum = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + INSURANCE_VALUES[item.type], 0);\n  const cap = insuranceSum * 2;\n  return { items, insuranceSum, cap, remainingCap: cap };\n}\n\nfunction computeDamagePayout(item: PolicyItem, damage: Damage): number {\n  const isHighEnchant = (item.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD;\n  const gross = isHighEnchant ? damage.amount * HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_FACTOR : damage.amount;\n  return gross - DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE;\n}\n\nexport function processClaim(\n  policy: Policy,\n  incident: Incident,\n): { payout: number; remainingCap: number } {\n  let totalRaw = 0;\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {\n    const item = policy.items.find((i) => i.type === damage.itemType);\n    if (!item) {\n      throw new Error(`Damage references item type not in policy: ${damage.itemType}`);\n    }\n    totalRaw += computeDamagePayout(item, damage);\n  }\n  const payout = Math.min(Math.floor(totalRaw), policy.remainingCap);\n  return { payout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout };\n}\n```\n\nHmm, extracting `HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD` and `HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_FACTOR` adds constants. In APP terms, that increases mass slightly. But it improves clarity. Let me weigh:\n\n- Pro: Names the threshold and factor explicitly. Self-documenting.\n- Con: Adds 2 constants and 2 bindings. Slight mass increase.\n\nActually, the `0.5` is intuitive (50% reduction). The `8` could use a name because \"enchantment >= 8\" is a magic threshold. But it's documented in the test name \"high enchantment clause\".\n\nLet me NOT extract these to constants - the inline values are clear enough.\n\nFinal refactor:\n```typescript\nfunction computeDamagePayout(item: PolicyItem, damage: Damage): number {\n  const isHighEnchant = (item.enchantment ?? 0) >= 8;\n  const gross = isHighEnchant ? damage.amount * 0.5 : damage.amount;\n  return gross - DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE;\n}\n\nexport function processClaim(...) {\n  let totalRaw = 0;\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {\n    const item = policy.items.find((i) => i.type === damage.itemType);\n    if (!item) {\n      throw new Error(`Damage references item type not in policy: ${damage.itemType}`);\n    }\n    totalRaw += computeDamagePayout(item, damage);\n  }\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nMass change:\n- Before: \n  - Constants: 13\n  - Bindings: 16\n  - Invocations: 6\n  - Conditionals: 2\n  - Loops: 3\n  - Assignments: 3\n  - Total: 13 + 16 + 12 + 8 + 15 + 18 = 82\n\n- After:\n  - Constants: 13 (same)\n  - Bindings: 16 + 2 (computeDamagePayout item, damage params, plus... wait, I need to re-count)\n    - Hmm, extracting means we still have the same bindings, just in a different function\n    - But there's now 1 extra function binding (computeDamagePayout function name)\n    - Actually the bindings move with the code: isHighEnchant and gross move into computeDamagePayout\n    - So bindings stay the same: 16. Plus 1 for computeDamagePayout function = 17\n  - Invocations: 6 + 1 (computeDamagePayout call) = 7\n  - Conditionals: 2 (same)\n  - Loops: 3 (same)\n  - Assignments: 3 (the `totalRaw += computeDamagePayout(...)` replaces `totalRaw += gross - DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE`, still 1 assignment)\n    - Wait, was it 3? Let me recount:\n      - totalRaw = 0: assignment 1\n      - cap = insuranceSum * 2: assignment 2\n      - totalRaw += gross - DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE: assignment 3\n    - Same: 3\n  - Total: 13 + 17 + 14 + 8 + 15 + 18 = 85\n\nHmm, the mass went UP by 3. That's because extracting added an invocation (computeDamagePayout call) and a function binding.\n\nBut Rule 2 (Reveals Intent) trumps APP. The extraction makes intent clearer.\n\nActually, wait. I should reconsider. Maybe there's a refactoring that reduces mass AND improves clarity.\n\nLet me think about using a `Map` for item lookup. If we build a Map once:\n```typescript\nconst itemMap = new Map(policy.items.map(item => [item.type, item]));\n```\n\nThen each find becomes O(1) instead of O(n). But for typical small policies this doesn't matter.\n\nLet me think about the reduce alternative:\n```typescript\nconst totalRaw = incident.damages.reduce((sum, damage) => {\n  const item = policy.items.find((i) => i.type === damage.itemType);\n  if (!item) {\n    throw new Error(`Damage references item type not in policy: ${damage.itemType}`);\n  }\n  return sum + computeDamagePayout(item, damage);\n}, 0);\n```\n\nThis eliminates the `let totalRaw = 0` initialization and the `totalRaw += ...` reassignment. But it adds a reduce invocation and closure.\n\nMass change:\n- Removes 2 assignments (init + update) = -12\n- Adds 1 invocation (reduce) = +2\n- Adds 1 closure binding (sum parameter) = +1 (but sum was already counted as a binding for `totalRaw`)\n\nHmm, this is getting complex. Let me just go with the extraction of `computeDamagePayout` and accept the slight mass increase for clarity.\n\nActually, let me think differently. The current code already has decent clarity. Maybe I should look for actual duplication or a less-good naming.\n\nLooking again at the code:\n```typescript\nexport function processClaim(\n  policy: Policy,\n  incident: Incident,\n): { payout: number; remainingCap: number } {\n  let totalRaw = 0;\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {\n    const item = policy.items.find((i) => i.type === damage.itemType);\n    if (!item) {\n      throw new Error(`Damage references item type not in policy: ${damage.itemType}`);\n    }\n    const isHighEnchant = (item.enchantment ?? 0) >= 8;\n    const gross = isHighEnchant ? damage.amount * 0.5 : damage.amount;\n    totalRaw += gross - DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE;\n  }\n  const payout = Math.min(Math.floor(totalRaw), policy.remainingCap);\n  return { payout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout };\n}\n```\n\nOne thing I notice: `find` returns the FIRST matching item. But what if there are multiple items of the same type? The find returns just one. Looking at the test \"policy with two swords, two sword damages → each damage treated independently\", this doesn't test the case where one damage refers to a type with multiple items - it tests two damages each finding one sword.\n\nWhat if a damage should fail because there are more damages of a type than items in policy? That's the it.todo \"more damages of a given type than items in policy → throws Error\". So we need to track which items have been claimed.\n\nHmm but that's a future test. For now, the current implementation works for the current tests.\n\nOK let me commit to the refactoring. I'll extract `computeDamagePayout`. The function name is important - it should reflect \"compute the contribution of a single damage to the raw payout\".\n\nActually, the name `computeDamagePayout` is a bit vague. Let me think:\n- `rawDamagePayout(item, damage)` - emphasizes \"raw\" (before cap)\n- `computeDamageContribution(item, damage)` - emphasizes contribution to total\n- `damagePayout(item, damage)` - simple and clear\n\nI'll go with `computeDamagePayout` - it's the most descriptive.\n\nOK let me also think about this: the `?? 0` for enchantment is defensive. Is it needed? Looking at the tests, all tests that use enchantment explicitly set it. None test undefined enchantment. But it's a good safety net. I'll keep it.\n\nFinal refactoring:\n1. Extract `computeDamagePayout` \n2. The processClaim loop body becomes much simpler\n\nLet me also consider: should I introduce `LOW_ENCHANTMENT_FACTOR = 1`? No, that's silly - the absence of a multiplier is more readable than multiplying by 1.\n\nOK, executing the refactoring now.\n\nActually wait, let me reconsider the entire approach. The instructions said the user wants me to invoke a \"subagent\" tool. Let me re-read.\n\nLooking at AGENTS.md: \"INVOKE SUBAGENT: Use the `subagent` tool with these parameters...\"\n\nBut I don't have a `subagent` tool. Let me check if it's available somewhere I missed. Looking at my tools: bash, edit, find, grep, ls, read, write. No subagent tool.\n\nSo I must do the refactoring myself. Let me proceed.\n\nI'll:\n1. Run tests first to confirm they pass\n2. Calculate current APP mass\n3. Apply refactoring (extract `computeDamagePayout`)\n4. Run tests to confirm they still pass\n5. Calculate new APP mass\n6. Document the changes\n\nLet me start.\n\nWait, the user message ended with \"Run autonomously, return when done.\" This suggests they want me to do the work and return a summary. I'll do the refactoring and report.\n\nLet me run the tests first to verify they pass.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"I'll perform the refactoring pass on `src/policy.ts`. Let me first verify the tests pass, then evaluate naming, calculate APP mass, and apply refactorings following the Four Rules of Simple Design."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_24dfbde8a01f4bb7a5daf069","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"pnpm test -- src/policy.spec.ts 2>&1 | tail -40"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/minimax-m3","usage":{"input":2048,"output":7944,"cacheRead":8064,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":7860,"totalTokens":18056,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784955750353,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-a1b5ffd4-a67a-43b0-95a5-7dba61fd916d","responseModel":"minimax/minimax-m3"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_24dfbde8a01f4bb7a5daf069","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n> tdd-experiment-run@ test /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_02-01-15_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2.1-phase-continuation-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking\n> vitest run src/policy.spec.ts\n\n\n RUN  v1.6.1 /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_02-01-15_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2.1-phase-continuation-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking\n\n ✓ src/policy.spec.ts  (21 tests | 3 skipped) 4ms\n\n Test Files  1 passed (1)\n      Tests  18 passed | 3 todo (21)\n   Start at  05:04:54\n   Duration  158ms (transform 29ms, setup 1ms, collect 26ms, tests 4ms, environment 0ms, prepare 41ms)\n\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784955895160},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"18 tests pass, 3 todo. Wait, the user said \"Passing tests: 11 (all createPolicy + many processClaim tests including itemType validation)\" but I'm seeing 18 passed. Let me re-count the tests:\n\ncreatePolicy: 6 tests\nprocessClaim: \n- 8 numbered tests + 1 \"throws Error\" test = 9 tests\n- 3 todo\n\nWait the test file shows:\ncreatePolicy: 6 tests (it blocks, all passing)\nprocessClaim: \n- \"steel sword enchantment 3...\" \n- \"rune damage 200...\"\n- \"steel sword enchantment 9...\"\n- \"dragon material sword enchantment 9...\"\n- \"dragon material sword enchantment 8...\"\n- \"dragon material sword enchantment 5...\"\n- \"dragon attack on sword...\"\n- \"policy with two swords...\"\n- \"single sword (cap 2000)...\"\n- \"single sword, two successive claims...\"\n- \"payout computation yielding 350.5...\"\n- \"damage itemType not in policy → throws Error\"\n- it.todo x3\n\nThat's 12 it blocks + 3 it.todo = 15 processClaim tests. Plus 6 createPolicy = 21 total. 18 passing, 3 todo. So 18 pass, 3 todo = 21 total. Good.\n\nThe user said \"Passing tests: 11 (all createPolicy + many processClaim tests including itemType validation)\". I count 6 createPolicy + 12 processClaim = 18 passing. The user might have miscounted, but the tests are passing. Let me proceed.\n\nActually wait, let me count the processClaim tests more carefully by reading the spec file:\n\n1. \"steel sword enchantment 3, damage 500...\"\n2. \"rune damage 200...\"\n3. \"steel sword enchantment 9...\"\n4. \"dragon material sword enchantment 9...\"\n5. \"dragon material sword enchantment 8...\"\n6. \"dragon material sword enchantment 5...\"\n7. \"dragon attack on sword...\"\n8. \"policy with two swords...\"\n9. \"single sword (cap 2000)...\"\n10. \"single sword, two successive claims...\"\n11. \"payout computation yielding 350.5...\"\n12. \"damage itemType not in policy...\"\n\nThat's 12 processClaim tests. Plus 6 createPolicy = 18 passing tests. The user said \"11\" but it's actually 18. Either way, all are passing.\n\nNow let me proceed with the refactoring. The current code:\n\n```typescript\n/**\n * Per-event deductible subtracted from every damage payout (in G).\n */\nconst DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE = 100;\n\n/**\n * Insurance values (in G) per item type. Per spec:\n *   sword=1000, amulet=600, staff=800, potion=400;\n *   components (rune/moonstone)=250 each.\n */\nconst INSURANCE_VALUES: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 1000,\n  amulet: 600,\n  staff: 800,\n  potion: 400,\n  rune: 250,\n  moonstone: 250,\n};\n\nexport type PolicyItem = {\n  type: string;\n  material?: string;\n  enchantment?: number;\n  cursed?: boolean;\n};\n\nexport type Policy = {\n  items: PolicyItem[];\n  insuranceSum: number;\n  cap: number;\n  remainingCap: number;\n};\n\nexport type Damage = { itemType: string; amount: number };\n\nexport type Incident = { cause: string; damages: Damage[] };\n\nexport function createPolicy(items: PolicyItem[]): Policy {\n  const insuranceSum = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + INSURANCE_VALUES[item.type], 0);\n  const cap = insuranceSum * 2;\n  return { items, insuranceSum, cap, remainingCap: cap };\n}\n\nexport function processClaim(\n  policy: Policy,\n  incident: Incident,\n): { payout: number; remainingCap: number } {\n  let totalRaw = 0;\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {\n    const item = policy.items.find((i) => i.type === damage.itemType);\n    if (!item) {\n      throw new Error(`Damage references item type not in policy: ${damage.itemType}`);\n    }\n    const isHighEnchant = (item.enchantment ?? 0) >= 8;\n    const gross = isHighEnchant ? damage.amount * 0.5 : damage.amount;\n    totalRaw += gross - DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE;\n  }\n  const payout = Math.min(Math.floor(totalRaw), policy.remainingCap);\n  return { payout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout };\n}\n```\n\n**Naming Evaluation (FIRST PRIORITY):**\n\n| Name | Current | Verdict |\n|------|---------|---------|\n| `createPolicy` | Creates a Policy from items | ✅ Clear, reveals intent |\n| `processClaim` | Processes an insurance claim | ✅ Clear, reveals intent |\n| `DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE` | The deductible subtracted per damage | ✅ Clear, reveals intent |\n| `INSURANCE_VALUES` | Map of item type to insurance value | ✅ Clear, reveals intent |\n| `PolicyItem`, `Policy`, `Damage`, `Incident` | Type definitions | ✅ Clear |\n| `totalRaw` | Total raw payout (before cap/flooring) | ⚠️ \"raw\" is a bit vague — \"raw\" relative to what? But contextually OK |\n| `gross` | Gross amount before deductible | ✅ Clear in context |\n| `isHighEnchant` | Is high enchantment? | ✅ Clear |\n| `item` (loop) | The matching policy item | ✅ Clear |\n| `i` (find callback) | The current item being checked | ⚠️ Could be `item` but `i` is idiomatic for find |\n\nNaming assessment: The naming is mostly clear. `totalRaw` could be clearer but it's understandable. No urgent rename needed.\n\n**Current APP Mass:**\n\nLet me carefully count:\n\n```typescript\n// constants in INSURANCE_VALUES: 1000, 600, 800, 400, 250, 250 → 6 constants\n// DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE = 100 → 1 constant\n\nexport function createPolicy(items: PolicyItem[]): Policy {\n  // 0 (reduce init) → 1 constant\n  // 2 (insuranceSum * 2) → 1 constant\n  const insuranceSum = items.reduce(...);\n  const cap = insuranceSum * 2;\n  return { items, insuranceSum, cap, remainingCap: cap };\n}\n\nexport function processClaim(...) {\n  let totalRaw = 0;                          // 1 constant (0)\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {\n    const item = policy.items.find(...);\n    if (!item) { throw new Error(...); }     // 1 conditional\n    const isHighEnchant = (item.enchantment ?? 0) >= 8;  // 0 (??0), 8 (>=8) → 2 constants\n    const gross = isHighEnchant ? damage.amount * 0.5 : damage.amount;  // 1 constant (0.5), 1 conditional\n    totalRaw += gross - DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE;\n  }\n  const payout = Math.min(Math.floor(totalRaw), policy.remainingCap);\n  return { payout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout };\n}\n```\n\nConstants: 6 (INSURANCE_VALUES) + 1 (DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE) + 1 (reduce init 0) + 1 (×2) + 1 (totalRaw init 0) + 2 (??0, >=8) + 1 (×0.5) = 13\nBindings: INSURANCE_VALUES(1), DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE(1), items(1), sum(1), item-reduce(1), insuranceSum(1), cap(1), policy(1), incident(1), totalRaw(1), damage(1), item-find(1), i(1), isHighEnchant(1), gross(1), payout(1) = 16\nInvocations: reduce(1), INSURANCE_VALUES[..](1), find(1), Error(1), Math.min(1), Math.floor(1) = 6\nConditionals: if(!item)(1), isHighEnchant?(1) = 2\nLoops: reduce(1), for(1), find(1) = 3\nAssignments: totalRaw=0(1), cap=insuranceSum*2(1), totalRaw+=(1) = 3\n\nTotal mass:\n- 13 × 1 = 13\n- 16 × 1 = 16\n- 6 × 2 = 12\n- 2 × 4 = 8\n- 3 × 5 = 15\n- 3 × 6 = 18\n\n**Total: 82**\n\n**Evaluate Simple Design Rules:**\n\nRule 1 (Tests pass): ✅ All 18 tests pass.\n\nRule 2 (Reveals intent): The `processClaim` body mixes three concerns:\n1. Looking up the policy item for each damage\n2. Validating the lookup\n3. Computing per-damage payout with the high-enchantment rule and deductible\n\nExtracting (3) into a named function would make the algorithm explicit: \"for each damage, find the matching item, then add its payout contribution\".\n\nRule 3 (No duplication): No obvious code duplication. The high-enchantment rule appears only once.\n\nRule 4 (Fewest elements): The current code has all necessary elements. The `isHighEnchant` and `gross` bindings are explaining variables — they aid Rule 2.\n\n**Decision: Apply Rule 2 refactoring — extract `computeDamagePayout(item, damage)`**\n\nThis names the high-enchantment rule and makes `processClaim` read as a clear algorithm.\n\nLet me apply the refactoring.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Tests pass (18 passing, 3 todo). Now let me perform the refactoring analysis.\n\n**Step 1: Naming Evaluation**\n\n| Name | Verdict |\n|------|---------|\n| `createPolicy`, `processClaim` | ✅ Clear, fit purpose |\n| `DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE`, `INSURANCE_VALUES` | ✅ Clear, named constants |\n| `totalRaw`, `gross`, `isHighEnchant` | ✅ Clear in context |\n| `i` (in `find` callback) | ✅ Idiomatic for filter/find |\n\nAll names still fit their purpose. No rename needed.\n\n**Step 2: Current APP Mass**\n\n```\nConstants: 13 (6 INSURANCE_VALUES entries + 1 DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE + 6 inline: 0, 0, 2, 0, 0, 8, 0.5)\nBindings: 16 (INSURANCE_VALUES, DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE, items, sum, item, insuranceSum, cap, policy, incident, totalRaw, damage, item, i, isHighEnchant, gross, payout)\nInvocations: 6 (reduce, INSURANCE_VALUES[..], find, Error, Math.min, Math.floor)\nConditionals: 2 (if !item, ternary isHighEnchant)\nLoops: 3 (reduce, for-of, find)\nAssignments: 3 (totalRaw=0, cap=ins*2, totalRaw+=)\n\nTotal = (13×1) + (16×1) + (6×2) + (2×4) + (3×5) + (3×6) = 13 + 16 + 12 + 8 + 15 + 18 = 82\n```\n\n**Step 3: Evaluate Simple Design Rules**\n\n- **Rule 1 (Tests pass):** ✅ 18/18 passing\n- **Rule 2 (Reveals intent):** `processClaim`'s body mixes three concerns (lookup, validate, compute). The high-enchantment rule is buried inside a 3-statement block. Extracting it names the algorithm.\n- **Rule 3 (No duplication):** No duplication found.\n- **Rule 4 (Fewest elements):** No unnecessary abstractions; bindings are explaining variables that serve Rule 2.\n\n**Decision: Apply Rule 2 — extract `computeDamagePayout(item, damage)`**\n\nThis names the high-enchantment clause explicitly so `processClaim` reads as a clear algorithm: validate, then accumulate per-damage contributions."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_8402a3928777429f93816735","name":"edit","arguments":{"edits":[{"newText":"function computeDamagePayout(item: PolicyItem, damage: Damage): number {\n  const isHighEnchant = (item.enchantment ?? 0) >= 8;\n  const gross = isHighEnchant ? damage.amount * 0.5 : damage.amount;\n  return gross - DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE;\n}\n\nexport function processClaim(\n  policy: Policy,\n  incident: Incident,\n): { payout: number; remainingCap: number } {\n  let totalRaw = 0;\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {\n    const item = policy.items.find((i) => i.type === damage.itemType);\n    if (!item) {\n      throw new Error(`Damage references item type not in policy: ${damage.itemType}`);\n    }\n    totalRaw += computeDamagePayout(item, damage);\n  }\n  const payout = Math.min(Math.floor(totalRaw), policy.remainingCap);\n  return { payout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout };\n}","oldText":"export function processClaim(\n  policy: Policy,\n  incident: Incident,\n): { payout: number; remainingCap: number } {\n  let totalRaw = 0;\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {\n    const item = policy.items.find((i) => i.type === damage.itemType);\n    if (!item) {\n      throw new Error(`Damage references item type not in policy: ${damage.itemType}`);\n    }\n    const isHighEnchant = (item.enchantment ?? 0) >= 8;\n    const gross = isHighEnchant ? damage.amount * 0.5 : damage.amount;\n    totalRaw += gross - DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE;\n  }\n  const payout = Math.min(Math.floor(totalRaw), policy.remainingCap);\n  return { payout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout };\n}"}],"path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_02-01-15_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2.1-phase-continuation-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/src/policy.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/minimax-m3","usage":{"input":232,"output":3142,"cacheRead":18048,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":2116,"totalTokens":21422,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784955895161,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-ef3014ed-4d99-426c-9a48-cc07391410fb","responseModel":"minimax/minimax-m3"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_8402a3928777429f93816735","toolName":"edit","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully replaced 1 b