b3547d84a276410fbd0b8e2c","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_02-01-15_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2.1-phase-continuation-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/src/policy.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/minimax-m3","usage":{"input":341,"output":109,"cacheRead":13952,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":22,"totalTokens":14402,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784956095065,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-cb723b7e-a0ff-459e-89c3-99f329fd01f3","responseModel":"minimax/minimax-m3"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_b3547d84a276410fbd0b8e2c","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"/**\n * Per-event deductible subtracted from every damage payout (in G).\n */\nconst DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE = 100;\n\n/**\n * Insurance values (in G) per item type. Per spec:\n *   sword=1000, amulet=600, staff=800, potion=400;\n *   components (rune/moonstone)=250 each.\n */\nconst INSURANCE_VALUES: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 1000,\n  amulet: 600,\n  staff: 800,\n  potion: 400,\n  rune: 250,\n  moonstone: 250,\n};\n\nexport type PolicyItem = {\n  type: string;\n  material?: string;\n  enchantment?: number;\n  cursed?: boolean;\n};\n\nexport type Policy = {\n  items: PolicyItem[];\n  insuranceSum: number;\n  cap: number;\n  remainingCap: number;\n};\n\nexport type Damage = { itemType: string; amount: number };\n\nexport type Incident = { cause: string; damages: Damage[] };\n\nexport function createPolicy(items: PolicyItem[]): Policy {\n  const insuranceSum = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + INSURANCE_VALUES[item.type], 0);\n  const cap = insuranceSum * 2;\n  return { items, insuranceSum, cap, remainingCap: cap };\n}\n\nfunction findPolicyItem(policy: Policy, itemType: string): PolicyItem {\n  const item = policy.items.find((i) => i.type === itemType);\n  if (!item) {\n    throw new Error(`Damage references item type not in policy: ${itemType}`);\n  }\n  return item;\n}\n\nfunction validateDamage(policy: Policy, damage: Damage): PolicyItem {\n  if (damage.amount < 0) {\n    throw new Error(`Damage amount cannot be negative: ${damage.amount}`);\n  }\n  return findPolicyItem(policy, damage.itemType);\n}\n\nfunction computeDamagePayout(item: PolicyItem, damage: Damage): number {\n  const isHighEnchant = (item.enchantment ?? 0) >= 8;\n  const gross = isHighEnchant ? damage.amount * 0.5 : damage.amount;\n  return gross - DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE;\n}\n\nexport function processClaim(\n  policy: Policy,\n  incident: Incident,\n): { payout: number; remainingCap: number } {\n  let totalRaw = 0;\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {\n    const item = validateDamage(policy, damage);\n    totalRaw += computeDamagePayout(item, damage);\n  }\n  const payout = Math.min(Math.floor(totalRaw), policy.remainingCap);\n  return { payout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout };\n}\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784956098083},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now let me do the new mass calculation and produce the final report.\n\n## Step 6: Calculate New APP Mass\n\nFor `processClaim`:\n- Constants (literals `0`, return object props 2): 3 × 1 = 3\n- Bindings (policy, incident, totalRaw, damage, item, payout): 6 × 1 = 6\n- Invocations (incident.damages, validateDamage, computeDamagePayout, Math.min, Math.floor, policy.remainingCap×2, -): 8 × 2 = 16\n- Conditionals: 0\n- Loops: 1 × 5 = 5\n- Assignments (=0, +=): 2 × 6 = 12\n\nTotal for processClaim: 42\n\nFor new helpers (added mass):\n`findPolicyItem`:\n- Bindings (policy, itemType, item, i): 4 × 1 = 4\n- Invocations (find, i.type===, Error, throw): 4 × 2 = 8\n- Conditionals (if !item): 4\n- Constants (error string): 1\nTotal: 17\n\n`validateDamage`:\n- Bindings (policy, damage): 2 × 1 = 2\n- Invocations (damage.amount, damage.amount, findPolicyItem): 3 × 2 = 6\n- Conditionals (if <0): 4\n- Constants (error string): 1\nTotal: 13\n\nWait, I need to also count `damage.amount < 0` which is both an invocation AND part of the conditional - let me count the conditional separately.\n\nActually `damage.amount` is an invocation (property access), and `<0` is a comparison. So:\n- damage.amount invocation: 2\n- damage.amount invocation (in error msg): 2  \n- findPolicyItem invocation: 2\n\nTotal mass for new code: 17 + 13 = 30 added in helpers\n\nMass before: ~63 for processClaim\nMass after: 42 (processClaim) + 30 (helpers) = 72\n\nHmm, mass actually went UP. But Rule 2 (clarity) trumps APP. Let me verify the clarity gain is real.\n\nThe loop body went from 3 mixed concerns (find, validate amount, compute) to 2 clear concerns (validate, compute):\n- Before: 7 lines in loop body, intermixed find+conditional+throw+conditional+throw+compute\n- After: 3 lines in loop body: validate, compute\n\nEach helper now has a single responsibility. `findPolicyItem` answers \"what is the policy item for this type?\" and `validateDamage` answers \"is this damage acceptable? if so, give me the item.\"\n\nThe clarity gain is worth the mass increase (Rule 2 trumps APP).\n\nLet me also evaluate Rule 3 (No Duplication):\n- Both helpers use similar throw-Error pattern, but they're distinct validations\n- Could extract a `requireOrThrow(cond, msg)` helper? That would be over-engineering for 2 callsites with different args.\n\nRule 4 (Fewest Elements):\n- We added 2 new functions\n- But each has a clear single purpose\n- The helpers are justified by the clarity gain in processClaim\n\nFinal verdict: Refactoring improves clarity (Rule 2) at the cost of slightly higher mass. This is correct per the priority rules.\n\nActually wait, let me reconsider. I made the refactoring MORE invasive than necessary. Let me consider whether extracting just `validateDamage` (without `findPolicyItem`) would have been simpler:\n\nOption A: Single `validateDamage` helper\n```typescript\nfunction validateDamage(policy: Policy, damage: Damage): PolicyItem {\n