return counts;\n}\n\nexport function processClaim(\n  policy: Policy,\n  incident: Incident,\n): { payout: number; remainingCap: number } {\n  const damageCounts = countBy(incident.damages, (d) => d.itemType);\n  const policyCounts = countBy(policy.items, (i) => i.type);\n  for (const [type, count] of damageCounts) {\n    const available = policyCounts.get(type) ?? 0;\n    if (count > available) {\n      throw new Error(\n        `More damages of type \"${type}\" (${count}) than items in policy (${available})`,\n      );\n    }\n  }\n  let totalRaw = 0;\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {\n    const item = validateDamage(policy, damage);\n    totalRaw += computeDamagePayout(item, damage);\n  }\n  const payout = Math.min(Math.floor(totalRaw), policy.remainingCap);\n  return { payout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout };\n}\n```\n\nWait, let me think more. Are there other improvements?\n\nLooking again at the code:\n\n1. `for (const [type, count] of damageCounts)` iterates only the keys in damageCounts. But what if `policy.items` has more types than damages? That's fine - we're checking `count > available`, which would be `0` if available is missing. Wait, `available = policyCounts.get(type) ?? 0` so if the type isn't in policyCounts, available is 0, which would throw if damages exist for a type not in policy. But that's already handled by `validateDamage` later.\n\nHmm, actually `validateDamage` already throws if the item isn't in the policy. So the count check is only meaningful for types that ARE in the policy. So our current logic:\n- If type is in damages but not in policy, `count > 0` would be true, throwing. This is fine.\n- If type is in both, checks count <= available.\n\nOK so the logic is sound. Let me think about whether there are any other improvements.\n\nActually, looking at this even more carefully, I notice:\n\nThe two count-by loops are duplicates. Extract them. Good.\n\nThen there's the validation logic:\n```typescript\nfor (const [type, count] of damageCounts) {\n  const available = policyCounts.get(type) ?? 0;\n  if (count > available) {\n    throw new Error(\n      `More damages of type \"${type}\" (${count}) than items in policy (${available})`,\n    );\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis could be extracted as a helper `validateDamageCounts(damageCounts, policyCounts)` for clarity. But that may be over-engineering for Rule 4 (fewest elements). The current inline version is readable.\n\nLet me also think about this: Is there any way to avoid the second loop (for totalRaw)? We could fold it with the count validation, but we'd need to know the count first. Hmm, actually we don't need to know count first if we validate as we go - but we'd need to track per-type counts.\n\nActually a cleaner refactoring might be to merge the two loops:\n1. First pass: build damageCounts\n2. Second pass: compute payouts (with validation done upfront or interleaved)\n\nOr we could check during the damage processing loop:\n\n```typescript\nconst used = new Map<string, number>();\nlet totalRaw = 0;\nfor (const damage of incident.damages) {\n  const item = validateDamage(policy, damage);\n  used.set(damage.itemType, (used.get(damage.itemType) ?? 0) + 1);\n  const available = policy.items.filter((i) => i.type === damage.itemType).length;\n  if (used.get(damage.itemType)! > available) {\n    throw new Error(`...`);\n  }\n  totalRaw += computeDamagePayout(item, damage);\n}\n```\n\nBut this is doing O(n*m) work for the filter, and is more complex. The current two-pass approach is cleaner.\n\nActually wait - we could avoid building policyCounts by computing the count differently, but it's basically the same.\n\nLet me think about the simplest improvement: extract `countBy` helper.\n\nActually I want to consider another angle: can we just iterate and check during the existing damage-processing loop?\n\nThe structure is:\n1. Validate count per type\n2. Process each damage and accumulate\n\nIf we fold them:\n\n```typescript\nconst damageCountPerType = new Map<string, number>();\nlet totalRaw = 0;\nfor (const damage of incident.damages) {\n  const item = validateDamage(policy, damage);\n  const newCount = (damageCountPerType.get(damage.itemType) ?? 0) + 1;\n  damageCountPerType.set(damage.itemType, newCount);\n  // Count available items of this type in policy\n  const available = policy.items.filter((i) => i.type === damage.itemType).length;\n  if (newCount > available) {\n    throw new Error(`...`);\n  }\n  totalRaw += computeDamagePayout(item, damage);\n}\n```\n\nBut the `policy.items.filter(...)` is O(n) per damage, making it O(n*m). The original approach is O(n+m). So this is worse for performance AND higher mass.\n\nLet me stick with the `countBy` extraction.\n\nAnother consideration: the `for ... of damageCounts` only iterates types present in damages. If we wanted to be more defensive, we could iterate all types. But it's correct as-is.\n\nLet me also consider: should I extract the count validation as a separate function?\n\n```typescript\nfunction validateDamageCounts(damageCounts: Map<string, number>, policyCounts: Map<string, number>): void {\n  for (const [type, count] of damageCounts) {\n    const available = policyCounts.get(type) ?? 0;\n    if (count > available) {\n      throw new Error(\n        `More damages of type \"${type}\" (${count}) than items in policy (${available})`,\n      );\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nHmm, this adds a function but the call site is shorter:\n```typescript\nvalidateDamageCounts(damageCounts, policyCounts);\n```\n\nI think extracting this would make processClaim cleaner. Let me weigh:\n\nWithout extraction (current refactored):\n```typescript\nconst damageCounts = countBy(incident.damages, (d) => d.itemType);\nconst policyCounts = countBy(policy.items, (i) => i.type);\nfor (const [type, count] of damageCounts) {\n  const available = policyCounts.get(type) ?? 0;\n  if (count > available) {\n    throw new Error(\n      `More damages of type \"${type}\" (${count}) than items in policy (${available})`,\n    );\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWith extraction:\n```typescript\nconst damageCounts = countBy(incident.damages, (d) => d.itemType);\nconst policyCounts = countBy(policy.items, (i) => i.type);\nvalidateDamageCounts(damageCounts, policyCounts);\n```\n\nThe extraction makes the main flow cleaner and the helper is reusable and well-named. I'll do both.\n\nActually, let me think one more time. Is `validateDamageCounts` truly necessary, or is it overkill?\n\nRule 4: Fewest Elements - \"Remove unnecessary abstractions\"\n\nThe validation block is 7 lines. Extracting it is justifiable because:\n1. It has a clear name that describes its purpose\n2. It can be tested independently\n3. It removes complexity from processClaim\n\nBut arguably, inline is fine because:\n1. The block is short\n2. The intent is clear from the code\n3. Adding a function adds an indirection\n\nI think keeping it inline is acceptable too. Let me extract just the `countBy` helper for now since that addresses clear duplication.\n\nActually, looking more carefully, I want to consider extracting `validateDamageCounts` because it would make `processClaim` much cleaner and reads like prose. Let me try both versions and decide.\n\nLet me think about mass:\n\n**Current code mass** (processClaim only): 93\n\n**Refactored with countBy extraction only**:\n- 2 explicit loops removed\n- Each had: 1 loop (5), 1 binding (1), 2 invocations (2*2=4), 1 conditional (4), 2 constants (2)\n- So removing 2 loops saves: 2*5 = 10 mass\n- Removing 2 inline bindings (damage, item) saves: 2 mass each\n- Wait, let me reconsider. The bindings `damage` and `item` were in those loops.\n\nActually the issue is that I need to recalculate, accounting for both removal of old code AND addition of new code (the helper).\n\n**Helper function `countBy`**:\n```typescript\nfunction countBy<T>(items: readonly T[], keyFn: (item: T) => string): Map<string, number> {\n  const counts = new Map<string, number>();\n  for (const item of items) {\n    const key = keyFn(item);\n    counts.set(key, (counts.get(key) ?? 0) + 1);\n  }\n  return counts;\n}\n```\n- `items` param = 1 binding\n- `keyFn` param = 1 binding\n- `counts` binding = 1\n- `new Map<...>()` = 1 invocation\n- `for ... of` = 1 loop (5)\n- `item` binding = 1\n- `key` binding = 1\n- `keyFn(item)` = 1 invocation\n- `counts.set(...)` = 1 invocation\n- `counts.get(...)` = 1 invocation\n- `??` = 1 conditional\n- `0` = 1 constant\n- `+ 1` = 0, `1` = 1 constant\n- `return` = 0\n\nTotal helper mass:\n- Bindings: 1+1+1+1+1 = 5 → 5\n- Constants: 1+1 = 2 → 2\n- Invocations: 1+1+1+1 = 4 → 8\n- Conditionals: 1 → 4\n- Loops: 1 → 5\n- Assignments: 0 → 0\n\nHelper total: 24\n\n**Refactored processClaim (with countBy calls instead of loops)**:\n```typescript\nexport function processClaim(\n  policy: Policy,\n  incident: Incident,\n): { payout: number; remainingCap: number } {\n  const damageCounts = countBy(incident.damages, (d) => d.itemType);\n  const policyCounts = countBy(policy.items, (i) => i.type);\n  for (const [type, count] of damageCounts) {\n    const available = policyCounts.get(type) ?? 0;\n    if (count > available) {\n      throw new Error(\n        `More damages of type \"${type}\" (${count}) than items in policy (${available})`,\n      );\n    }\n  }\n  let totalRaw = 0;\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {\n    const item = validateDamage(policy, damage);\n    totalRaw += computeDamagePayout(item, damage);\n  }\n  const payout = Math.min(Math.floor(totalRaw), policy.remainingCap);\n  return { payout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout };\n}\n```\n\n- Parameters: `policy`, `incident` = 2 bindings\n- `damageCounts` = 1 binding\n- `countBy(...)` invocation = 1 invocation\n- arrow function `(d) => d.itemType` = 1 binding `d`, 1 invocation `d.itemType` (property access). Actually a lambda has its own binding... but it's quite lightweight. Let me count it as 1 binding.\n- Hmm, should I count the arrow function's binding? The lambda itself is an invocation (creating a function). Let me count: 1 invocation (creating the lambda), 1 binding (d).\n- `policyCounts` = 1 binding\n- `countBy(...)` invocation = 1 invocation\n- arrow function `(i) => i.type` = 1 invocation, 1 binding\n- `for ... of damageCounts` = 1 loop (5), 2 bindings (type, count)\n- `available` = 1 binding\n- `policyCounts.get(type)` = 1 invocation\n- `?? 0` = 1 conditional, 1 constant\n- `if (count > available)` = 1 conditional\n- `new Error(...)` = 1 invocation\n- `let totalRaw = 0` = 1 binding, 1 constant, 1 assignment\n- `for (const damage of incident.damages)` = 1 loop, 1 binding\n- `const item = validateDamage(...)` = 1 binding, 1 invocation\n- `totalRaw += ...` = 1 assignment, 1 invocation\n- `const payout = Math.min(...)` = 1 binding, 2 invocations\n\nBindings: 2 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 2 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 15\nConstants: 1 + 1 = 2\nInvocations: 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 2 + 1 = 10\nConditionals: 1 + 1 + 1 = 3\nLoops: 1 + 1 = 2\nAssignments: 1 + 1 = 2\n\nMass:\n- Bindings: 15\n- Constants: 2\n- Invocations: 10*2 = 20\n- Conditionals: 3*4 = 12\n- Loops: 2*5 = 10\n- Assignments: 2*6 = 12\n\nTotal: 15 + 2 + 20 + 12 + 10 + 12 = 71\n\nPlus helper: 24\nTotal new mass: 71 + 24 = 95\n\nWait, that's higher than 93. Let me recount the original.\n\nActually, I think I miscounted the original. Let me redo carefully.\n\n**Original processClaim** (lines only, no comments):\n```typescript\nexport function processClaim(\n  policy: Policy,\n  incident: Incident,\n): { payout: number; remainingCap: number } {\n  const damageCounts = new Map<string, number>();\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {\n    damageCounts.set(damage.itemType, (damageCounts.get(damage.itemType) ?? 0) + 1);\n  }\n  const policyCounts = new Map<string, number>();\n  for (const item of policy.items) {\n    policyCounts.set(item.type, (policyCounts.get(item.type) ?? 0) + 1);\n  }\n  for (const [type, count] of damageCounts) {\n    const available = policyCounts.get(type) ?? 0;\n    if (count > available) {\n      throw new Error(\n        `More damages of type \"${type}\" (${count}) than items in policy (${available})`,\n      );\n    }\n  }\n  let totalRaw = 0;\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {\n    const item = validateDamage(policy, damage);\n    totalRaw += computeDamagePayout(item, damage);\n  }\n  const payout = Math.min(Math.floor(totalRaw), policy.remainingCap);\n  return { payout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout };\n}\n```\n\nLine 1: `export function processClaim(`\nLine 2: `  policy: Policy,` → 1 binding (policy)\nLine 3: `  incident: Incident,` → 1 binding (incident)\nLine 5: `  const damageCounts = new Map<string, number>();` → 1 binding (damageCounts), 1 invocation (new Map)\nLine 6: `  for (const damage of incident.damages) {` → 1 loop, 1 binding (damage)\nLine 7: `    damageCounts.set(damage.itemType, (damageCounts.get(damage.itemType) ?? 0) + 1);` → 2 invocations (set, get), 1 conditional (??), 2 constants (0, 1)\nLine 9: `  const policyCounts = new Map<string, number>();` → 1 binding (policyCounts), 1 invocation (new Map)\nLine 10: `  for (const item of policy.items) {` → 1 loop, 1 binding (item)\nLine 11: `    policyCounts.set(item.type, (policyCounts.get(item.type) ?? 0) + 1);` → 2 invocations (set, get), 1 conditional (??), 2 constants (0, 1)\nLine 13: `  for (const [type, count] of damageCounts) {` → 1 loop, 2 bindings (type, count)\nLine 14: `    const available = policyCounts.get(type) ?? 0;` → 1 binding (available), 1 invocation (get), 1 conditional (??), 1 constant (0)\nLine 15: `    if (count > available) {` → 1 conditional (if)\nLine 16: `      throw new Error(` → 1 invocation (new Error)\nLine 20: `  let totalRaw = 0;` → 1 binding (totalRaw), 1 constant (0), 1 assignment\nLine 21: `  for (const damage of incident.damages) {` → 1 loop, 1 binding (damage)\nLine 22: `    const item = validateDamage(policy, damage);` → 1 binding (item), 1 invocation (validateDamage)\nLine 23: `    totalRaw += computeDamagePayout(item, damage);` → 1 assignment, 1 invocation (computeDamagePayout)\nLine 25: `  const payout = Math.min(Math.floor(totalRaw), policy.remainingCap);` → 1 binding (payout), 2 invocations (Math.min, Math.floor)\n\nCounting:\n- Bindings: 2 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 2 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 14\n- Constants: 2 + 2 + 1 + 1 = 6\n- Invocations: 1 + 2 + 1 + 2 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 2 = 12\n- Conditionals: 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 4\n- Loops: 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 4\n- Assignments: 1 + 1 = 2\n\nMass:\n- Bindings: 14 × 1 = 14\n- Constants: 6 × 1 = 6\n- Invocations: 12 × 2 = 24\n- Conditionals: 4 × 4 = 16\n- Loops: 4 × 5 = 20\n- Assignments: 2 × 6 = 12\n\nTotal: 14 + 6 + 24 + 16 + 20 + 12 = 92\n\nNow the refactored version:\n\n```typescript\nexport function processClaim(\n  policy: Policy,\n  incident: Incident,\n): { payout: number; remainingCap: number } {\n  const damageCounts = countBy(incident.damages, (d) => d.itemType);\n  const policyCounts = countBy(policy.items, (i) => i.type);\n  for (const [type, count] of damageCounts) {\n    const available = policyCounts.get(type) ?? 0;\n    if (count > available) {\n      throw new Error(\n        `More damages of type \"${type}\" (${count}) than items in policy (${available})`,\n      );\n    }\n  }\n  let totalRaw = 0;\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {\n    const item = validateDamage(policy, damage);\n    totalRaw += computeDamagePayout(item, damage);\n  }\n  const payout = Math.min(Math.floor(totalRaw), policy.remainingCap);\n  return { payout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout };\n}\n```\n\n- Line 2-3: parameters `policy`, `incident` = 2 bindings\n- Line 5: `const damageCounts = countBy(...)` = 1 binding, 1 invocation (countBy)\n- `(d) => d.itemType` = arrow function: 1 invocation (creating), 1 binding (d), no other (itemType is property access)\n- Line 6: `const policyCounts = countBy(...)` = 1 binding, 1 invocation\n- `(i) => i.type` = 1 invocation, 1 binding\n- Line 7: `for (const [type, count] of damageCounts) {` = 1 loop, 2 bindings\n- Line 8: `const available = ...` = 1 binding, 1 invocation (get), 1 conditional (??), 1 constant (0)\n- Line 9: `if (count > available) {` = 1 conditional\n- Line 10: `throw new Error(...)` = 1 invocation\n- Line 14: `let totalRaw = 0;` = 1 binding, 1 constant, 1 assignment\n- Line 15: `for (const damage of incident.damages) {` = 1 loop, 1 binding\n- Line 16: `const item = validateDamage(...)` = 1 binding, 1 invocation\n- Line 17: `totalRaw += ...` = 1 assignment, 1 invocation\n- Line 19: `const payout = Math.min(Math.floor(...))` = 1 binding, 2 invocations\n\nBindings: 2 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 2 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 13\nConstants: 1 + 1 = 2\nInvocations: 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 2 = 9\nConditionals: 1 + 1 + 1 = 3\nLoops: 1 + 1 = 2\nAssignments: 1 + 1 = 2\n\nMass:\n- Bindings: 13\n- Constants: 2\n- Invocations: 9 × 2 = 18\n- Conditionals: 3 × 4 = 12\n- Loops: 2 × 5 = 10\n- Assignments: 2 × 6 = 12\n\nprocessClaim mass: 13 + 2 + 18 + 12 + 10 + 12 = 67\n\nNow the countBy helper:\n```typescript\nfunction countBy<T>(items: readonly T[], keyFn: (item: T) => string): Map<string, number> {\n  const counts = new Map<string, number>();\n  for (const item of items) {\n    const key = keyFn(item);\n    counts.set(key, (counts.get(key) ?? 0) + 1);\n  }\n  return counts;\n}\n```\n- Line 1: parameters `items`, `keyFn` = 2 bindings\n- Line 2: `const counts = new Map<...>()` = 1 binding, 1 invocation\n- Line 3: `for (const item of items) {` = 1 loop, 1 binding\n- Line 4: `const key = keyFn(item);` = 1 binding, 1 invocation\n- Line 5: `counts.set(key, (counts.get(key) ?? 0) + 1);` = 2 invocations, 1 conditional, 2 constants\n\nBindings: 2 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 5\nConstants: 2\nInvocations: 1 + 1 + 2 = 4\nConditionals: 1\nLoops: 1\n\nMass: 5 + 2 + 8 + 4 + 5 = 24\n\nTotal new mass (processClaim + countBy): 67 + 24 = 91\n\nSo the total mass went from 92 to 91. Marginal improvement.\n\nHmm, that's only 1 unit saved. The clear duplication is gone but the mass barely changes. Let me think if there's a way to do better.\n\nWait, I realize I was double-counting arrow function as both invocation and binding. An arrow function literal like `(d) => d.itemType` when passed as an argument - is the creation of the function an invocation? In JavaScript terms, it's an evaluation that produces a function value. I think it's debatable, but for our mass purposes, we should probably count it as an invocation (creating the function). But also, the binding `d` should only count if it's meaningful.\n\nActually, I think lambdas add very little value to count, and including them in mass analysis would obscure the comparison. Let me re-think...\n\nActually let me ignore arrow functions in my mass calculation. The internal binding `d` is barely a binding - it's just a parameter declaration.\n\nHmm, but parameter declarations ARE bindings in JS terms. OK let me just count them as bindings.\n\nActually, you know what, I think I should be more pragmatic. The duplication is real and the extraction is justified even if mass is similar. Let me focus on clarity benefits.\n\nLet me think about the value of refactoring more holistically:\n\n1. **DRY**: The two count-building loops are clearly duplicated. Extracting countBy is a clear win.\n2. **Clarity**: After extraction, the intent is clearer - \"count by itemType\" vs \"manually building a map\".\n3. **Maintainability**: If we ever need to change the count logic (e.g., handle nulls), there's one place to do it.\n\nI think this is a good refactoring. Let me also consider extracting `validateDamageCounts`:\n\n```typescript\nfunction validateDamageCounts(\n  damageCounts: Map<string, number>,\n  policyCounts: Map<string, number>,\n): void {\n  for (const [type, count] of damageCounts) {\n    const available = policyCounts.get(type) ?? 0;\n    if (count > available) {\n      throw new Error(\n        `More damages of type \"${type}\" (${count}) than items in policy (${available})`,\n      );\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThen processClaim becomes:\n```typescript\nexport function processClaim(\n  policy: Policy,\n  incident: Incident,\n): { payout: number; remainingCap: number } {\n  validateDamageCounts(\n    countBy(incident.damages, (d) => d.itemType),\n    countBy(policy.items, (i) => i.type),\n  );\n  let totalRaw = 0;\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {\n    const item = validateDamage(policy, damage);\n    totalRaw += computeDamagePayout(item, damage);\n  }\n  const payout = Math.min(Math.floor(totalRaw), policy.remainingCap);\n  return { payout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout };\n}\n```\n\nBut this passes two maps to a function only used once. The local variables `damageCounts` and `policyCounts` are now gone. Hmm, this might be borderline Rule 4.\n\nLet me think about whether processClaim is now easier to read:\n\nBefore:\n```typescript\nconst damageCounts = new Map<string, number>();\nfor (const damage of incident.damages) {\n  damageCounts.set(damage.itemType, (damageCounts.get(damage.itemType) ?? 0) + 1);\n}\nconst policyCounts = new Map<string, number>();\nfor (const item of policy.items) {\n  policyCounts.set(item.type, (policyCounts.get(item.type) ?? 0) + 1);\n}\nfor (const [type, count] of damageCounts) {\n  const available = policyCounts.get(type) ?? 0;\n  if (count > available) {\n    throw new Error(\n      `More damages of type \"${type}\" (${count}) than items in policy (${available})`,\n    );\n  }\n}\n```\n\nAfter (countBy only):\n```typescript\nconst damageCounts = countBy(incident.damages, (d) => d.itemType);\nconst policyCounts = countBy(policy.items, (i) => i.type);\nfor (const [type, count] of damageCounts) {\n  const available = policyCounts.get(type) ?? 0;\n  if (count > available) {\n    throw new Error(\n      `More damages of type \"${type}\" (${count}) than items in policy (${available})`,\n    );\n  }\n}\n```\n\nAfter (countBy + validateDamageCounts):\n```typescript\nvalidateDamageCounts(\n  countBy(incident.damages, (d) => d.itemType),\n  countBy(policy.items, (i) => i.type),\n);\n```\n\nThe third version is more concise but loses the named intermediate variables. Hmm.\n\nActually, I'll go with the middle version: extract `countBy` but keep the validation logic inline. This is the cleanest balance.\n\nWait, let me reconsider. Looking again:\n\n```typescript\nconst damageCounts = countBy(incident.damages, (d) => d.itemType);\nconst policyCounts = countBy(policy.items, (i) => i.type);\nfor (const [type, count] of damageCounts) {\n  const available = policyCounts.get(type) ?? 0;\n  if (count > available) {\n    throw new Error(\n      `More damages of type \"${type}\" (${count}) than items in policy (${available})`,\n    );\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe block from `for ... of damageCounts` through the throw is about 8 lines. It's a self-contained chunk with a clear purpose. Extracting it is reasonable.\n\nHmm, I'll go with extracting `validateDamageCounts` too. The trade-off: one more function, but processClaim becomes a clean top-level flow:\n\n1. Validate counts\n2. Compute totalRaw\n3. Return payout\n\nThis reads more like prose. I think this is the better refactoring.\n\nActually wait, I want to reconsider one more time. The Rule 4 says \"Remove unnecessary abstractions\". Is extracting validateDamageCounts necessary?\n\nLooking at the function: it's called once, with self-evident logic. Extracting doesn't add much. But it does:\n- Make processClaim read top-to-bottom as a sequence of high-level steps\n- Give a name to the validation logic\n- Make the validation potentially reusable (e.g., for pre-flight checks elsewhere)\n\nI'll extract it.\n\nLet me also think about whether the damage processing loop should be extracted:\n\n```typescript\nlet totalRaw = 0;\nfor (const damage of incident.damages) {\n  const item = validateDamage(policy, damage);\n  totalRaw += computeDamagePayout(item, damage);\n}\n```\n\nThis is the core logic. It's clear. It uses `totalRaw` as an accumulator across iterations, which is harder to express as a one-liner without losing the explicit reduction. I could use `incident.damages.reduce(...)` but the imperative form is arguably clearer here. Let me leave this alone.\n\nActually, can I use reduce?\n\n```typescript\nconst totalRaw = incident.damages.reduce(\n  (sum, damage) => sum + computeDamagePayout(validateDamage(policy, damage), damage),\n  0\n);\n```\n\nThis is a single expression. But it loses the explicit named steps. Hmm, I'd say the current form is fine. Let me not over-refactor.\n\nActually wait, this might be a real improvement. Let me think:\n\nCurrent:\n```typescript\nlet totalRaw = 0;\nfor (const damage of incident.damages) {\n  const item = validateDamage(policy, damage);\n  totalRaw += computeDamagePayout(item, damage);\n}\n```\n\nMass:\n- `let totalRaw = 0`: 1 binding, 1 constant, 1 assignment\n- `for (const damage of ...)`: 1 loop, 1 binding\n- `const item = validateDamage(policy, damage)`: 1 binding, 1 invocation\n- `totalRaw += computeDamagePayout(item, damage)`: 1 assignment, 1 invocation\n\nBindings: 4, Constants: 1, Invocations: 2, Conditionals: 0, Loops: 1, Assignments: 2\nMass: 4 + 1 + 4 + 0 + 5 + 12 = 26\n\nWith reduce:\n```typescript\nconst totalRaw = incident.damages.reduce(\n  (sum, damage) => sum + computeDamagePayout(validateDamage(policy, damage), damage),\n  0\n);\n```\n\n- `totalRaw` binding: 1\n- `incident.damages.reduce(...)`: 1 invocation\n- Lambda `(sum, damage) => ...`: 1 invocation, 2 bindings\n- `sum + ...`: arithmetic\n- `computeDamagePayout(...)`: 1 invocation\n- `validateDamage(...)`: 1 invocation\n- `0` initial: 1 constant\n\nBindings: 1 + 2 = 3\nConstants: 1\nInvocations: 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 4\nConditionals: 0\nLoops: 0 (reduce internally loops but is an invocation)\nAssignments: 0\n\nMass: 3 + 1 + 8 + 0 + 0 + 0 = 12\n\nThat's much lower mass! And arguably as clear, maybe even clearer because it expresses the intent (\"sum these\") rather than the mechanics.\n\nHmm, but `validateDamage` inside the lambda - will it throw? Yes, and reduce will propagate that. OK.\n\nLet me consider: is `reduce` clearer than the imperative loop here?\n\nPros of reduce:\n- Shorter, lower mass\n- Expresses intent directly: \"reduce damages to a sum of payouts\"\n- No mutation\n\nCons of reduce:\n- Lambda adds indirection\n- Implicit loop (might be less obvious to less experienced readers)\n\nI think in TypeScript, `reduce` is idiomatic. And the lambda is short.\n\nLet me try this approach:\n\n```typescript\nexport function processClaim(\n  policy: Policy,\n  incident: Incident,\n): { payout: number; remainingCap: number } {\n  validateDamageCounts(\n    countBy(incident.damages, (d) => d.itemType),\n    countBy(policy.items, (i) => i.type),\n  );\n  const totalRaw = incident.damages.reduce(\n    (sum, damage) => sum + computeDamagePayout(validateDamage(policy, damage), damage),\n    0\n  );\n  const payout = Math.min(Math.floor(totalRaw), policy.remainingCap);\n  return { payout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout };\n}\n```\n\nThis is much cleaner!\n\nHmm wait, let me reconsider. The lambda inside reduce captures `policy`. So `validateDamage(policy, damage)` is called inside the lambda. The `validateDamage` function is not really part of the \"data transformation\" of reduce; it's a validation side effect. Mixing validation into reduce might be confusing.\n\nBut that's how it has to be - the validation is interleaved with the processing because each damage needs to be validated against policy.\n\nOK let me check if `validateDamage` could be moved out. Currently:\n```typescript\nfunction validateDamage(policy: Policy, damage: Damage): PolicyItem {\n  const item = policy.items.find((i) => i.type === damage.itemType);\n  if (!item) {\n    throw new Error(`Damage references item type not in policy: ${damage.itemType}`);\n  }\n  if (damage.amount < 0) {\n    throw new Error(`Damage amount cannot be negative: ${damage.amount}`);\n  }\n  return item;\n}\n```\n\nThis validates each damage individually. We could move these checks earlier in processClaim:\n\n```typescript\nexport function processClaim(...) {\n  validateDamageCounts(...);\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {\n    validateDamage(policy, damage); // validates and throws, but doesn't use the item\n  }\n  const totalRaw = incident.damages.reduce(\n    (sum, damage) => sum + computeDamagePayout(...),\n    0\n  );\n}\n```\n\nBut this duplicates work. `validateDamage` already does `policy.items.find(...)` for each damage. Doing it twice is wasteful.\n\nActually, `validateDamageCounts` already ensures that for each type in damages, there are at least count items in policy. But it doesn't ensure that the itemType exists at all (if type is in damages but not in policy, count > 0 vs available = 0 would throw). Wait, yes it does - if `count > 0` and `available = 0`, it throws. So actually `validateDamageCounts` does cover the \"type not in policy\" case! \n\nBut it doesn't cover the \"amount < 0\" case. And it doesn't catch duplicates in the count check (since damages is the array, duplicates are counted).\n\nHmm so we'd still need to validate amount < 0. But validateDamage handles both. If we keep validateDamage inside the reduce, we get both checks.\n\nAlternatively, we could split validateDamage into two:\n- validateDamageExists (the find check)\n- validateDamageAmount (the negative check)\n\nOr rename it to something clearer. But this is starting to feel like over-engineering.\n\nLet me step back. The current state is:\n- countBy duplication → extract countBy (clear win)\n- count validation logic → could extract, but adds a function for one use\n- damage processing loop → could use reduce for lower mass\n\nI think the minimum impactful refactoring is:\n1. Extract `countBy` to remove duplication\n2. (Optional) Extract `validateDamageCounts` for cleaner processClaim\n\nLet me also consider the reduce option. The trade-off:\n- reduce: lower mass, more declarative, but mixes validation and computation\n- imperative loop: explicit, but more mass\n\nI'll go with the imperative loop for the damage processing because:\n1. The validation `validateDamage` is a meaningful side effect (throws)\n2. The imperative form makes the validation explicit\n3. The mass difference is acceptable\n\nActually wait, looking again at validateDamage:\n\n```typescript\nfunction validateDamage(policy: Policy, damage: Damage): PolicyItem {\n  const item = policy.items.find((i) => i.type === damage.itemType);\n  if (!item) {\n    throw new Error(`Damage references item type not in policy: ${damage.itemType}`);\n  }\n  if (damage.amount < 0) {\n    throw new Error(`Damage amount cannot be negative: ${damage.amount}`);\n  }\n  return item;\n}\n```\n\nIt returns the PolicyItem. Why? Because in the current code:\n```typescript\nconst item = validateDamage(policy, damage);\ntotalRaw += computeDamagePayout(item, damage);\n```\n\nThe caller uses the returned item. So validateDamage serves double duty: validates AND looks up the item.\n\nCould we refactor to separate these concerns?\n\nOption A: Keep validateDamage as is, caller uses returned item.\nOption B: Split into `findItem(policy, type)` and `validateDamage(item, damage)`.\nOption C: Inline the lookup into computeDamagePayout, keep validateDamage for validation only.\n\nOption C is interesting. Let me think:\n\n```typescript\nfunction computeDamagePayout(policy: Policy, damage: Damage): number {\n  const item = policy.items.find((i) => i.type === damage.itemType);\n  if (!item) {\n    throw new Error(`Damage references item type not in policy: ${damage.itemType}`);\n  }\n  if (damage.amount < 0) {\n    throw new Error(`Damage amount cannot be negative: ${damage.amount}`);\n  }\n  const isHighEnchant = (item.enchantment ?? 0) >= 8;\n  const gross = isHighEnchant ? damage.amount * 0.5 : damage.amount;\n  return gross - DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE;\n}\n```\n\nBut this would mean looking up the item twice in some cases (once in validateDamageCounts and once here). Actually wait, no - validateDamageCounts builds a map of counts, not items. So we'd do the lookups twice. That's wasteful.\n\nHmm, but actually, validateDamageCounts already throws if any damage's type isn't in policy. So the lookup in computeDamagePayout is guaranteed to succeed (if we passed validateDamageCounts first). So... the lookup is essentially redundant if we trust validateDamageCounts.\n\nBut validateDamageCounts only throws if `count > available` for any type. If count is 0, no throw. If a damage's type isn't in policy, count is 1, available is 0, throws. OK so it's covered.\n\nAnd the negative amount check isn't covered. So we still need that.\n\nLet me think of a cleaner organization:\n\nOption D: Move validation logic into validateDamageCounts (validate count + negative amounts).\n\n```typescript\nfunction validateDamageCounts(\n  damages: Damage[],\n  policyCounts: Map<string, number>,\n): void {\n  const damageCounts = countBy(damages, (d) => d.itemType);\n  for (const [type, count] of damageCounts) {\n    const available = policyCounts.get(type) ?? 0;\n    if (count > available) {\n      throw new Error(\n        `More damages of type \"${type}\" (${count}) than items in policy (${available})`,\n      );\n    }\n  }\n  for (const damage of damages) {\n    if (damage.amount < 0) {\n      throw new Error(`Damage amount cannot be negative: ${damage.amount}`);\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThen in processClaim:\n```typescript\nvalidateDamageCounts(incident.damages, countBy(policy.items, (i) => i.type));\nconst totalRaw = incident.damages.reduce(\n  (sum, damage) => sum + computeDamagePayout(policy, damage),\n  0\n);\n```\n\nAnd computeDamagePayout becomes:\n```typescript\nfunction computeDamagePayout(policy: Policy, damage: Damage): number {\n  const item = policy.items.find((i) => i.type === damage.itemType)!;\n  const isHighEnchant = (item.enchantment ?? 0) >= 8;\n  const gross = isHighEnchant ? damage.amount * 0.5 : damage.amount;\n  return gross - DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE;\n}\n```\n\nBut this changes the responsibility of computeDamagePayout to do its own lookup. And we'd still do the lookup inside. Plus, the lookup happens for each damage (m lookups where m = number of damages), even though we already validated counts.\n\nHmm, this is getting complex. Let me back off and stick with simpler refactorings.\n\nOK my plan:\n1. Extract `countBy` helper to remove duplication\n2. Keep `validateDamage` and `computeDamagePayout` as they are\n3. Keep `processClaim` structure similar, just with `countBy` replacing the manual loops\n4. Keep the count validation logic inline (don't extract `validateDamageCounts` since it's only used once and is short)\n\nLet me also think about naming one more time. Are there improvements?\n\n- `processClaim` - clear\n- `createPolicy` - clear\n- `validateDamage` - clear, returns the item for convenience\n- `computeDamagePayout` - clear\n- `DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE` - clear\n- `INSURANCE_VALUES` - clear\n\nInside processClaim:\n- `damageCounts`, `policyCounts` - clear\n- `available`, `count` - clear\n- `totalRaw` - clear (\"total raw amount before rounding/capping\")\n\nNaming looks good. No renames needed.\n\n## Decision\n\nI'll do one refactoring: extract `countBy` helper to remove duplication between the damage-count and policy-count loops.\n\nLet me make the change.\n\nActually wait, let me also consider whether to extract `validateDamageCounts`. Pros and cons:\n\nPros:\n- Makes processClaim read more like a high-level narrative\n- Gives a name to the validation logic\n- Could be reused for other validation paths\n\nCons:\n- Used only once\n- Adds a function definition (minor)\n- Adds an abstraction layer\n\nI think it's a small win to extract it. Let me extract both `countBy` and `validateDamageCounts`. The result will be cleaner processClaim.\n\nHmm, actually let me decide based on the mass. Let me compute the mass of the version with both extractions:\n\n```typescript\nexport function processClaim(\n  policy: Policy,\n  incident: Incident,\n): { payout: number; remainingCap: number } {\n  validateDamageCounts(\n    countBy(incident.damages, (d) => d.itemType),\n    countBy(policy.items, (i) => i.type),\n  );\n  let totalRaw = 0;\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {\n    const item = validateDamage(policy, damage);\n    totalRaw += computeDamagePayout(item, damage);\n  }\n  const payout = Math.min(Math.floor(totalRaw), policy.remainingCap);\n  return { payout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout };\n}\n```\n\n- Parameters: 2 bindings\n- `validateDamageCounts(...)` = 1 invocation\n- `countBy(...)` = 1 invocation (each, but two of them) = 2 invocations\n- 2 arrow functions = 2 invocations (creating), 2 bindings (d, i) - but minor\n\nWait, do I need to count the inline arrow functions? Let me ignore them for simplicity (they're trivial).\n\n- `validateDamageCounts`: 1 invocation (1×2=2)\n- `countBy(...)` x 2: 2 invocations (2×2=4)\n- `let totalRaw = 0`: 1 binding, 1 constant, 1 assignment\n- `for (const damage of ...)`: 1 loop, 1 binding\n- `const item = validateDamage(policy, damage)`: 1 binding, 1 invocation\n- `totalRaw += computeDamagePayout(item, damage)`: 1 assignment, 1 invocation\n- `const payout = Math.min(Math.floor(totalRaw), policy.remainingCap)`: 1 binding, 2 invocations\n\nBindings: 2 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 6\nConstants: 1\nInvocations: 1 + 2 + 1 + 1 + 2 = 7\nConditionals: 0\nLoops: 1\nAssignments: 2\n\nMass: 6 + 1 + 14 + 0 + 5 + 12 = 38\n\nCompare to current (92). Big improvement in mass!\n\nNow for `validateDamageCounts`:\n```typescript\nfunction validateDamageCounts(\n  damageCounts: Map<string, number>,\n  policyCounts: Map<string, number>,\n): void {\n  for (const [type, count] of damageCounts) {\n    const available = policyCounts.get(type) ?? 0;\n    if (count > available) {\n      throw new Error(\n        `More damages of type \"${type}\" (${count}) than items in policy (${available})`,\n      );\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n- 2 parameters = 2 bindings\n- 1 loop, 2 bindings\n- 1 binding (available), 1 invocation (get), 1 conditional (??), 1 constant (0)\n- 1 conditional (if)\n- 1 invocation (new Error)\n\nBindings: 2 + 2 + 1 = 5\nConstants: 1\nInvocations: 1 + 1 = 2\nConditionals: 1 + 1 = 2\nLoops: 1\n\nMass: 5 + 1 + 4 + 8 + 5 = 23\n\ncountBy (from before): 24\n\nTotal new mass: 38 + 23 + 24 = 85\n\nvs original 92.\n\nSo extracting both reduces mass from 92 to 85. That's a 7-unit reduction.\n\nAnd the clarity benefit is clear.\n\nOK let me go with both extractions.\n\nActually, hmm, let me reconsider once more. Should the damage processing loop also use reduce?\n\nWith reduce:\n```typescript\nexport function processClaim(\n  policy: Policy,\n  incident: Incident,\n): { payout: number; remainingCap: number } {\n  validateDamageCounts(\n    countBy(incident.damages, (d) => d.itemType),\n    countBy(policy.items, (i) => i.type),\n  );\n  const totalRaw = incident.damages.reduce(\n    (sum, damage) => sum + computeDamagePayout(validateDamage(policy, damage), damage),\n    0\n  );\n  const payout = Math.min(Math.floor(totalRaw), policy.remainingCap);\n  return { payout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout };\n}\n```\n\nMass:\n- Parameters: 2 bindings\n- validateDamageCounts: 1 invocation\n- countBy x 2: 2 invocations\n- `totalRaw` binding: 1\n- `incident.damages.reduce`: 1 invocation\n- Lambda creation: 1 invocation\n- Lambda: `sum`, `damage` bindings: 2\n- `sum + computeDamagePayout(...)`: 1 invocation\n- `validateDamage(policy, damage)`: 1 invocation\n- `0` constant: 1\n- `payout` binding: 1\n- `Math.min(Math.floor(...))`: 2 invocations\n\nBindings: 2 + 1 + 2 + 1 = 6\nConstants: 1\nInvocations: 1 + 2 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 2 = 9\nConditionals: 0\nLoops: 0\nAssignments: 0\n\nMass: 6 + 1 + 18 + 0 + 0 + 0 = 25\n\nThat's a big mass reduction in processClaim! But the lambda adds an extra invocation and 2 bindings (not many).\n\nLet me also factor in the helper functions:\n- countBy: 24\n- validateDamageCounts: 23\n\nTotal with reduce: 25 + 24 + 23 = 72\n\nvs original: 92. That's a 20-unit reduction!\n\nAnd clarity is improved - the imperative loop was 4 lines and now it's 4 lines of reduce. Let me compare them again:\n\nOriginal imperative:\n```typescript\nlet totalRaw = 0;\nfor (const damage of incident.damages) {\n  const item = validateDamage(policy, damage);\n  totalRaw += computeDamagePayout(item, damage);\n}\n```\n\nRefactored reduce:\n```typescript\nconst totalRaw = incident.damages.reduce(\n  (sum, damage) => sum + computeDamagePayout(validateDamage(policy, damage), damage),\n  0\n);\n```\n\nThe reduce version expresses intent more directly. \"Sum these damage payouts\". The imperative version says \"loop and accumulate\". \n\nTrade-off: reduce adds an extra function invocation (the lambda) and nests computeDamagePayout inside validateDamage, which is harder to read.\n\nI think the reduce is fine. TypeScript developers are familiar with it. Let me use reduce.\n\nActually, you know what, let me be more conservative. The refactor is for the recently added count validation logic. Let me focus on that and leave the existing damage-processing loop alone. Mixing too many changes at once isn't ideal for the refactor.\n\nBut wait - the task says \"Refactor the implementation while keeping all tests green.\" It doesn't restrict me to only the new code. And the rules say to evaluate naming, DRY, etc. for all relevant code.\n\nOK let me commit to:\n1. Extract `countBy` helper\n2. Extract `validateDamageCounts` helper\n3. Convert damage processing loop to reduce\n\nActually, let me reconsider the reduce. The advantage of the imperative loop is that the named variable `item` makes the code more readable. With reduce, we have to nest:\n\n```typescript\n(sum, damage) => sum + computeDamagePayout(validateDamage(policy, damage), damage)\n```\n\nThis is a bit dense. But still readable.\n\nOK let me try the reduce approach and see.\n\nHmm actually let me think about this differently. The rule of thumb is \"extract until you can't extract no more, but not further\". Right now:\n\n1. `countBy` - clearly a reusable abstraction, definitely extract\n2. `validateDamageCounts` - one-time use but gives processClaim clarity, marginal extraction\n3. `reduce` instead of imperative loop - lower mass but more dense\n\nLet me go with 1 and 2, keep 3 as imperative. The mass is similar (85 vs 72), but the imperative loop with `item` binding is more readable than the nested reduce.\n\nFinal plan:\n1. Extract `countBy<T>` helper\n2. Extract `validateDamageCounts` helper\n3. Keep `processClaim` structure similar but with these helpers\n\nLet me also check if there's any way to make the code more readable using named intermediate variables. For example, instead of inline arrow functions in `countBy`, we could pass a string property name. But that's more abstract and less clear. I'll stick with the keyFn approach.\n\nWait, one more thought: should countBy take an array or readonly array? And should it use `Object.groupBy` or similar built-ins? Let me check.\n\nActually `Object.groupBy` was added in ES2024 and might not be widely available. It also returns a plain object, not a Map. Let me stick with the simple Map-based approach.\n\nLet me also reconsider if I should keep countBy as a Map. Maybe a plain object `{[type]: count}` is simpler? But then we'd need to handle the case where a type is \"__proto__\" or similar (prototype pollution). Map is safer.\n\nOK, committing to the plan. Let me implement it.\n\nOne more consideration: should countBy be exported? In this small module, it's only used internally. Let me keep it private.\n\nLet me write the refactored code:\n\n```typescript\n/**\n * Per-event deductible subtracted from every damage payout (in G).\n */\nconst DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE = 100;\n\n/**\n * Insurance values (in G) per item type. Per spec:\n *   sword=1000, amulet=600, staff=800, potion=400;\n *   components (rune/moonstone)=250 each.\n */\nconst INSURANCE_VALUES: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 1000,\n  amulet: 600,\n  staff: 800,\n  potion: 400,\n  rune: 250,\n  moonstone: 250,\n};\n\nexport type PolicyItem = {\n  type: string;\n  material?: string;\n  enchantment?: number;\n  cursed?: boolean;\n};\n\nexport type Policy = {\n  items: PolicyItem[];\n  insuranceSum: number;\n  cap: number;\n  remainingCap: number;\n};\n\nexport type Damage = { itemType: string; amount: number };\n\nexport type Incident = { cause: string; damages: Damage[] };\n\nexport function createPolicy(items: PolicyItem[]): Policy {\n  const insuranceSum = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + INSURANCE_VALUES[item.type], 0);\n  const cap = insuranceSum * 2;\n  return { items, insuranceSum, cap, remainingCap: cap };\n}\n\nfunction validateDamage(policy: Policy, damage: Damage): PolicyItem {\n  const item = policy.items.find((i) => i.type === damage.itemType);\n  if (!item) {\n    throw new Error(`Damage references item type not in policy: ${damage.itemType}`);\n  }\n  if (damage.amount < 0) {\n    throw new Error(`Damage amount cannot be negative: ${damage.amount}`);\n  }\n  return item;\n}\n\nfunction computeDamagePayout(item: PolicyItem, damage: Damage): number {\n  const isHighEnchant = (item.enchantment ?? 0) >= 8;\n  const gross = isHighEnchant ? damage.amount * 0.5 : damage.amount;\n  return gross - DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE;\n}\n\nfunction countBy<T>(items: readonly T[], keyFn: (item: T) => string): Map<string, number> {\n  const counts = new Map<string, number>();\n  for (const item of items) {\n    const key = keyFn(item);\n    counts.set(key, (counts.get(key) ?? 0) + 1);\n  }\n  return counts;\n}\n\nfunction validateDamageCounts(\n  damageCounts: Map<string, number>,\n  policyCounts: Map<string, number>,\n): void {\n  for (const [type, count] of damageCounts) {\n    const available = policyCounts.get(type) ?? 0;\n    if (count > available) {\n      throw new Error(\n        `More damages of type \"${type}\" (${count}) than items in policy (${available})`,\n      );\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nexport function processClaim(\n  policy: Policy,\n  incident: Incident,\n): { payout: number; remainingCap: number } {\n  validateDamageCounts(\n    countBy(incident.damages, (d) => d.itemType),\n    countBy(policy.items, (i) => i.type),\n  );\n  let totalRaw = 0;\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {\n    const item = validateDamage(policy, damage);\n    totalRaw += computeDamagePayout(item, damage);\n  }\n  const payout = Math.min(Math.floor(totalRaw), policy.remainingCap);\n  return { payout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout };\n}\n```\n\nWait, in the validateDamageCounts I'm passing two Map arguments. Let me think if this is clean. Alternative:\n\n```typescript\nfunction validateDamageCounts(policy: Policy, damages: readonly Damage[]): void {\n  const damageCounts = countBy(damages, (d) => d.itemType);\n  const policyCounts = countBy(policy.items, (i) => i.type);\n  for (const [type, count] of damageCounts) {\n    const available = policyCounts.get(type) ?? 0;\n    if (count > available) {\n      throw new Error(\n        `More damages of type \"${type}\" (${count}) than items in policy (${available})`,\n      );\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis is more cohesive - the function takes policy and damages (the two things it needs) and computes the counts internally. The signature is cleaner.\n\nLet me check usage:\n```typescript\nvalidateDamageCounts(policy, incident.damages);\n```\n\nvs previous:\n```typescript\nvalidateDamageCounts(\n  countBy(incident.damages, (d) => d.itemType),\n  countBy(policy.items, (i) => i.type),\n);\n```\n\nThe first is much cleaner. Let me go with that.\n\nNow mass for validateDamageCounts (new version):\n```typescript\nfunction validateDamageCounts(policy: Policy, damages: readonly Damage[]): void {\n  const damageCounts = countBy(damages, (d) => d.itemType);\n  const policyCounts = countBy(policy.items, (i) => i.type);\n  for (const [type, count] of damageCounts) {\n    const available = policyCounts.get(type) ?? 0;\n    if (count > available) {\n      throw new Error(\n        `More damages of type \"${type}\" (${count}) than items in policy (${available})`,\n      );\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n- Parameters: `policy`, `damages` = 2 bindings\n- `damageCounts` binding: 1, `countBy(...)` invocation: 1\n- arrow `(d) => d.itemType`: 1 invocation (creating), 1 binding (d)\n- `policyCounts` binding: 1, `countBy(...)` invocation: 1\n- arrow `(i) => i.type`: 1 invocation (creating), 1 binding (i)\n- `for (const [type, count] of damageCounts)`: 1 loop, 2 bindings\n- `const available = policyCounts.get(type) ?? 0`: 1 binding, 1 invocation, 1 conditional, 1 constant\n- `if (count > available)`: 1 conditional\n- `throw new Error(...)`: 1 invocation\n\nBindings: 2 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 2 + 1 = 9\nConstants: 1\nInvocations: 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 6\nConditionals: 1 + 1 = 2\nLoops: 1\n\nMass: 9 + 1 + 12 + 8 + 5 = 35\n\nHmm that's higher than my earlier calculation (23). The inline version was lower mass because the countBy calls happened at the call site.\n\nLet me weigh clarity vs mass:\n- Inline (in processClaim): processClaim has countBy calls + validation loop = 38 mass for processClaim + 23 mass for validateDamageCounts = 61 total\n- Cohesive (takes policy+damages): processClaim = 22 mass (just the call + rest) + 35 mass for validateDamageCounts = 57 total\n\nWait let me recompute processClaim mass for the cohesive version:\n```typescript\nexport function processClaim(\n  policy: Policy,\n  incident: Incident,\n): { payout: number; remainingCap: number } {\n  validateDamageCounts(policy, incident.damages);\n  let totalRaw = 0;\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {\n    const item = validateDamage(policy, damage);\n    totalRaw += computeDamagePayout(item, damage);\n  }\n  const payout = Math.min(Math.floor(totalRaw), policy.remainingCap);\n  return { payout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout };\n}\n```\n\n- Parameters: 2 bindings\n- `validateDamageCounts(policy, incident.damages)`: 1 invocation\n- `let totalRaw = 0`: 1 binding, 1 constant, 1 assignment\n- `for (const damage of incident.damages)`: 1 loop, 1 binding\n- `const item = validateDamage(policy, damage)`: 1 binding, 1 invocation\n- `totalRaw += computeDamagePayout(item, damage)`: 1 assignment, 1 invocation\n- `const payout = Math.min(Math.floor(totalRaw), policy.remainingCap)`: 1 binding, 2 invocations\n\nBindings: 2 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 6\nConstants: 1\nInvocations: 1 + 1 + 1 + 2 = 5\nConditionals: 0\nLoops: 1\nAssignments: 2\n\nMass: 6 + 1 + 10 + 0 + 5 + 12 = 34\n\nTotal with cohesive validateDamageCounts: 34 + 35 + 24 = 93\n\nHmm, that's about the same as the original (92). So the cohesive version doesn't help mass.\n\nLet me try the inline-arrow style for countBy:\n```typescript\nvalidateDamageCounts(\n  countBy(incident.damages, (d) => d.itemType),\n  countBy(policy.items, (i) => i.type),\n);\n```\n\nprocessClaim mass:\n- 2 params + 1 invocation + 2 invocations + 2 arrow creations + 2 bindings (d, i) + 2 bindings (damageCounts, policyCounts) = ?\n\nWait, I had this before. Let me just go with the cohesive version - the call site is cleaner even if total mass is similar.\n\nActually, with the cohesive version, processClaim becomes:\n```typescript\nvalidateDamageCounts(policy, incident.damages);\nlet totalRaw = 0;\nfor (const damage of incident.damages) {\n  const item = validateDamage(policy, damage);\n  totalRaw += computeDamagePayout(item, damage);\n}\nconst payout = Math.min(Math.floor(totalRaw), policy.remainingCap);\nreturn { payout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout };\n```\n\nThis reads like:\n1. Validate damage counts against policy\n2. Compute raw total\n3. Apply cap and return\n\nVery clean! The cohesive version of validateDamageCounts is the winner.\n\nLet me also consider the inline arrows issue. We could pre-define the key selectors:\n```typescript\nconst keyByItemType = (d: Damage) => d.itemType;\nconst keyByItemType2 = (i: PolicyItem) => i.type;\n```\n\nBut that's ugly. Arrow literals inline are fine.\n\nOK let me commit to the cohesive version. Let me also think about whether countBy should be inlined into validateDamageCounts since it's only used by that function. Hmm, but then validateDamageCounts would do too much. Let me keep countBy separate.\n\nActually wait - countBy could also be used in `createPolicy`! No, createPolicy uses reduce to sum, not to count. So no other use.\n\nBut countBy is a useful generic helper that could be reused later. Let me keep it as a separate function.\n\nOK final plan:\n1. Extract `countBy<T>` (generic helper)\n2. Extract `validateDamageCounts(policy, damages)` (cohesive helper)\n3. processClaim becomes a clean high-level flow\n\nLet me also think about TypeScript: the helper functions need proper types. Let me make sure the types are correct.\n\n```typescript\nfunction countBy<T>(items: readonly T[], keyFn: (item: T) => string): Map<string, number> {\n  const counts = new Map<string, number>();\n  for (const item of items) {\n    const key = keyFn(item);\n    counts.set(key, (counts.get(key) ?? 0) + 1);\n  }\n  return counts;\n}\n```\n\n`readonly T[]` is fine. `keyFn: (item: T) => string` is fine. Returns Map<string, number>. Good.\n\n```typescript\nfunction validateDamageCounts(policy: Policy, damages: readonly Damage[]): void {\n  const damageCounts = countBy(damages, (d) => d.itemType);\n  const policyCounts = countBy(policy.items, (i) => i.type);\n  for (const [type, count] of damageCounts) {\n    const available = policyCounts.get(type) ?? 0;\n    if (count > available) {\n      throw new Error(\n        `More damages of type \"${type}\" (${count}) than items in policy (${available})`,\n      );\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nLooks good.\n\nLet me also remove the comment that's now redundant:\n- `// Count damages per item type and compare against policy items count.` - This comment is no longer needed since the function name `validateDamageCounts` says it all.\n\nOK let me implement this.\n\nActually, one more thought: should I rename `validateDamage` since it has a slightly different concern now? It validates a single damage AND returns the looked-up item. That's two things. Should I:\na) Rename to `lookupAndValidateDamage` (more descriptive but ugly)\nb) Split into `findPolicyItem` and `validateDamage`\nc) Leave as is (works fine)\n\nI'll leave as is - it's used in one place, and the dual purpose is local and clear.\n\nNow let me write the final code:\n\n```typescript\n/**\n * Per-event deductible subtracted from every damage payout (in G).\n */\nconst DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE = 100;\n\n/**\n * Insurance values (in G) per item type. Per spec:\n *   sword=1000, amulet=600, staff=800, potion=400;\n *   components (rune/moonstone)=250 each.\n */\nconst INSURANCE_VALUES: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 1000,\n  amulet: 600,\n  staff: 800,\n  potion: 400,\n  rune: 250,\n  moonstone: 250,\n};\n\nexport type PolicyItem = {\n  type: string;\n  material?: string;\n  enchantment?: number;\n  cursed?: boolean;\n};\n\nexport type Policy = {\n  items: PolicyItem[];\n  insuranceSum: number;\n  cap: number;\n  remainingCap: number;\n};\n\nexport type Damage = { itemType: string; amount: number };\n\nexport type Incident = { cause: string; damages: Damage[] };\n\nexport function createPolicy(items: PolicyItem[]): Policy {\n  const insuranceSum = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + INSURANCE_VALUES[item.type], 0);\n  const cap = insuranceSum * 2;\n  return { items, insuranceSum, cap, remainingCap: cap };\n}\n\nfunction validateDamage(policy: Policy, damage: Damage): PolicyItem {\n  const item = policy.items.find((i) => i.type === damage.itemType);\n  if (!item) {\n    throw new Error(`Damage references item type not in policy: ${damage.itemType}`);\n  }\n  if (damage.amount < 0) {\n    throw new Error(`Damage amount cannot be negative: ${damage.amount}`);\n  }\n  return item;\n}\n\nfunction computeDamagePayout(item: PolicyItem, damage: Damage): number {\n  const isHighEnchant = (item.enchantment ?? 0) >= 8;\n  const gross = isHighEnchant ? damage.amount * 0.5 : damage.amount;\n  return gross - DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE;\n}\n\nfunction countBy<T>(items: readonly T[], keyFn: (item: T) => string): Map<string, number> {\n  const counts = new Map<string, number>();\n  for (const item of items) {\n    const key = keyFn(item);\n    counts.set(key, (counts.get(key) ?? 0) + 1);\n  }\n  return counts;\n}\n\nfunction validateDamageCounts(policy: Policy, damages: readonly Damage[]): void {\n  const damageCounts = countBy(damages, (d) => d.itemType);\n  const policyCounts = countBy(policy.items, (i) => i.type);\n  for (const [type, count] of damageCounts) {\n    const available = policyCounts.get(type) ?? 0;\n    if (count > available) {\n      throw new Error(\n        `More damages of type \"${type}\" (${count}) than items in policy (${available})`,\n      );\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nexport function processClaim(\n  policy: Policy,\n  incident: Incident,\n): { payout: number; remainingCap: number } {\n  validateDamageCounts(policy, incident.damages);\n  let totalRaw = 0;\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {\n    const item = validateDamage(policy, damage);\n    totalRaw += computeDamagePayout(item, damage);\n  }\n  const payout = Math.min(Math.floor(totalRaw), policy.remainingCap);\n  return { payout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout };\n}\n```\n\nLet me also calculate the new total mass to compare:\n\ncountBy mass: 24\nvalidateDamageCounts mass: 35\nprocessClaim mass: 34\nSum: 93\n\nHmm, same as before. Let me recount carefully.\n\nOriginal processClaim:\n- 14 bindings (1×14=14)\n- 6 constants (1×6=6)\n- 12 invocations (2×12=24)\n- 4 conditionals (4×4=16)\n- 4 loops (5×4=20)\n- 2 assignments (6×2=12)\n- Total: 92\n\nRefactored processClaim (with countBy + validateDamageCounts extracted, cohesive signature):\n- 6 bindings (1×