r moves but the count stays the same.\n- Function split means we have one more function invocation (`validateDamageAmount`). Adds 2 mass.\n\nSo mass increases slightly but clarity improves. Rule 2 trumps APP.\n\nLet me verify by running the tests after the refactor.\n\nWait, also consider: the comment \"raw payout for a single damage event, before flooring and cap adjustment\" was actually missing before. Let me re-check the original code:\n\n```typescript\nfunction computeDamagePayout(item: PolicyItem, damage: Damage): number {\n  const isHighEnchant = (item.enchantment ?? 0) >= 8;\n  const gross = isHighEnchant ? damage.amount * 0.5 : damage.amount;\n  return gross - DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE;\n}\n```\n\nNo comment. I could add a comment but it's fine - the name is descriptive.\n\nOK, let me also think: should I unify the two `find` operations (count check and per-damage lookup)? Building a single map once could be more efficient. But for this small example, the current code is fine and clear.\n\nActually, wait. Let me look at this differently. The current code structure has:\n1. `validateDamageCounts` runs BEFORE the loop\n2. `validateDamage` runs INSIDE the loop (looks up item + validates amount)\n\nThe count validation can throw with a meaningful error before any work is done. Then per-damage validation handles individual cases. This is actually a good design pattern.\n\nLet me commit to the refactoring and run the tests.\n\nActually, let me also think about whether to extract more. Let me look at what's clean in quote.ts:\n\n```typescript\nfunction basePremiumFor(type: string): number {\n  if (!(type in BASE_PREMIUMS)) {\n    throw new Error(`Unknown item type: ${type}`);\n  }\n  return BASE_PREMIUMS[type];\n}\n```\n\nquote.ts has a separate `basePremiumFor` function. policy.ts uses `INSURANCE_VALUES[item.type]` directly. For consistency with quote.ts, I could add `insuranceValueFor` but this would only be used in one place. The current inline access is fine.\n\nLet me also check: is there a way to verify my refactor preserves behavior? Yes - I run the tests.\n\nOK, let me also think about whether `cursed` and `material` are used. Looking at the tests, they ARE tested - the tests pass with these fields in PolicyItem. But the implementation doesn't reference them anywhere! That's because the tests show that cursed and material don't affect the claim payout (only the high-enchantment clause does).\n\nSo `cursed` and `material` are part of PolicyItem type but not used in `processClaim`. Should I remove them from PolicyItem? No, because:\n- The tests use them when creating policies\n- They might be referenced in the future\n- They're optional fields anyway\n\nActually wait, looking at this more carefully. The `PolicyItem` type has:\n- `type` (required)\n- `material` (optional)\n- `enchantment` (optional)\n- `cursed` (optional)\n\nIn `computeDamagePayout`, only `enchantment` is used. In `findPolicyItem`, only `type` is used. So `material` and `cursed` are unused.\n\nBut removing them would break the test compilation. Let me check:\n- Test: `{ type: \"sword\", material: \"dragon\", enchantment: 9 }` - uses material\n- Test: `{ type: \"sword\", cursed: true }` - uses cursed\n\nSo these fields ARE used in test inputs. The type needs to keep them. But they're unused in implementation. That's OK - it's the type for future use.\n\nOK, my refactoring plan is solid. Let me execute it.\n\nActually wait, one more thought: the `findPolicyItem` function uses `find` which is O(n). We could use a Map for O(1) lookup. But this is over-engineering for the current case. Skip.\n\nLet me also think about whether `computeDamagePayout` should validate the input. Currently it doesn't - it just does arithmetic. The caller is responsible for validating the amount. This is fine.\n\nNow let me also think about another small refactoring:\n\nThe current `processClaim`:\n```typescript\nexport function processClaim(\n  policy: Policy,\n  incident: Incident,\n): { payout: number; remainingCap: number } {\n  validateDamageCounts(policy, incident.damages);\n  let totalRaw = 0;\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {\n    const item = validateDamage(policy, damage);\n    totalRaw += computeDamagePayout(item, damage);\n  }\n  const payout = Math.min(Math.floor(totalRaw), policy.remainingCap);\n  return { payout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout };\n}\n```\n\nThis is pretty clear. The two phases (validate all counts, then process each) are evident. Maybe could be:\n```typescript\nexport function processClaim(policy, incident) {\n  validateDamageCounts(policy, incident.damages);\n  const totalRaw = sumDamagePayouts(policy, incident.damages);\n  const payout = Math.min(Math.floor(totalRaw), policy.remainingCap);\n  return { payout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout };\n}\n\nfunction sumDamagePayouts(policy, damages) {\n  let total = 0;\n  for (const damage of damages) {\n    const item = findPolicyItem(policy, damage);\n    validateDamageAmount(damage);\n    total += computeDamagePayout(item, damage);\n  }\n  return total;\n}\n```\n\nThis is more decomposed. But adds a function call (mass 2). Probably not worth it. Skip.\n\nOK, let me commit to the refactoring.\n\nWait, one more thought. Let me re-read what was said:\n\n> Recent Green phase: Activated unknown itemType test which already passes thanks to count validation.\n\nThis is interesting - the unknown itemType test passes because count validation catches it (1 damage of \"broomstick\" but 0 in policy). But it's also caught by `findPolicyItem` (no item with type \"broomstick\"). The `validateDamageCounts` runs FIRST, so it throws first with its specific error message.\n\nWait, let me check the test:\n```typescript\nit(\"damage with unknown itemType → throws Error\", () => {\n    const policy = createPolicy([{ type: \"sword\" }]);\n    expect(() => processClaim(policy, { cause: \"test\", damages: [{ itemType: \"broomstick\", amount: 200 }] })).toThrow();\n});\n```\n\nIt just expects `.toThrow()` (any error). So either error would pass. But in the current code, `validateDamageCounts` runs first because it's called before the loop. So it throws with \"More damages of type 'broomstick' (1) than items in policy (0)\".\n\nThis is a bit of a semantic issue. The \"unknown itemType\" should arguably throw \"item type not in policy\" rather than \"more damages than items\". But the test passes either way.\n\nShould I change the order so that find-throws comes first? That might be cleaner:\n```typescript\n// First validate all items exist\nfor (const damage of damages) {\n  findPolicyItem(policy, damage);\n}\n// Then validate counts\nvalidateDamageCounts(policy, damages);\n// Then process\n```\n\nBut this adds a full pass over damages. Hmm.\n\nActually, the current design has a good reason: count validation gives better error messages for the count case, and per-item validation handles other issues. And the test is generic.\n\nI won't change the order.\n\nOK, final plan: extract magic numbers + split validation. Let me execute.\n\nActually, let me also re-read the comment in policy.ts:\n\n```typescript\n/**\n * Insurance values (in G) per item type. Per spec:\n *   sword=1000, amulet=600, staff=800, potion=400;\n *   components (rune/moonstone)=250 each.\n */\n```\n\nThis is a good docstring style. I should match this for the new constants.\n\nOK let me write the refactored code.\n\nWait, one more consideration: should I also remove `computeDamagePayout`'s `isHighEnchant` variable? It's used twice but in a small expression. Let me see:\n\n```typescript\nfunction computeDamagePayout(item: PolicyItem, damage: Damage): number {\n  const isHighEnchant = (item.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD;\n  const gross = isHighEnchant ? damage.amount * HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_FACTOR : damage.amount;\n  return gross - DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE;\n}\n```\n\nThe variable `isHighEnchant` is an explaining variable. Per Rule 2, this is GOOD - it makes the intent clear. Keep it.\n\nSimilarly `gross` is an explaining variable. Keep.\n\nOK final refactored code coming up.\n\nLet me also think about whether to add a comment to `processClaim` describing the algorithm:\n\n```typescript\n/**\n * Process a claim against a policy:\n *   1. Verify no damage type is over-counted vs policy items.\n *   2. Sum the per-damage payouts (high-enchant clause applied, deductible subtracted).\n *   3. Floor the sum and clamp to the remaining cap.\n */\nexport function processClaim(...)\n```\n\nThis adds clarity. Rule 2.\n\nOK, let me finalize.\n\nActually, one more thought: let me think about the dedupe concern. The two `findPolicyItem` and `validateDamageCounts` both iterate over damages. Could we share work?\n\n```typescript\nfunction processAllDamages(policy, damages) {\n  const itemMap = new Map<string, PolicyItem[]>();\n  for (const item of policy.items) {\n    const list = itemMap.get(item.type) ?? [];\n    list.push(item);\n    itemMap.set(item.type, list);\n  }\n  \n  const damageCounts = new Map<string, number>();\n  let totalRaw = 0;\n  for (const damage of damages) {\n    validateDamageAmount(damage);\n    const items = itemMap.get(damage.itemType);\n    if (!items || items.length === 0) {\n      throw new Error(`Damage references item type not in policy: ${damage.itemType}`);\n    }\n    const used = (damageCounts.get(damage.itemType) ?? 0) + 1;\n    if (used > items.length) {\n      throw new Error(`More damages of type \"${damage.itemType}\" (${used}) than items in policy (${items.length})`);\n    }\n    damageCounts.set(damage.itemType, used);\n    totalRaw += computeDamagePayout(items[used - 1], damage);\n  }\n  // ...\n}\n```\n\nHmm, this is more complex but more efficient. But it's over-engineering for the task. The current design with two passes is fine and clear. Stick with the simple refactoring.\n\nOK, final refactoring:\n\n1. Add `HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD = 8` and `HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_FACTOR = 0.5` constants with docstrings.\n2. Split `validateDamage` into `findPolicyItem` and `validateDamageAmount`.\n3. Add a brief docstring to `processClaim` explaining the algorithm.\n4. Update `computeDamagePayout` to use the new constants and add a docstring.\n\nLet me execute this.\n\nActually, on reflection, let me also think about whether the change to constants is purely cosmetic (just renames) or if it has real value.\n\nMagic number `8` in context: \"is the high enchantment clause applies when enchantment >= 8?\" - unclear without context.\nNamed constant `HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD`: instantly clear.\n\nThis is a clear Rule 2 improvement. Keep.\n\nMagic number `0.5` in context: \"50% reimbursement\" - the meaning is somewhat clear, but naming it `HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_FACTOR` (or `HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_REIMBURSEMENT_RATE`) makes it explicit.\n\nKeep.\n\nOK, let me execute.\n\nOne concern: the user said \"Passing tests: 14 (all createPolicy and processClaim tests done)\". Wait, but my test run showed 21 tests passing in policy.spec.ts. The 14 might refer to a different count. Let me not worry about it - 21 tests pass, that's what matters.\n\nActually looking at the test file, count:\n- describe(\"createPolicy\"): 6 tests\n- describe(\"processClaim\"): 15 tests\nTotal: 21\n\nThe user said 14 - maybe they're counting differently (e.g., not counting the test cases as separate). Anyway, what matters is all tests pass.\n\nLet me execute the refactoring.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"I'll start by reading the implementation, test file, and related context, then analyze refactoring opportunities."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_5ff7060e76794ca39c5a7d5a","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"pnpm test 2>&1 | tail -15"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/minimax-m3","usage":{"input":1713,"output":8169,"cacheRead":10496,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":8113,"totalTokens":20378,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784956680639,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-42ff4198-f467-49fb-ab50-d458215f44f5","responseModel":"minimax/minimax-m3"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_5ff7060e76794ca39c5a7d5a","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"> tdd-experiment-run@ test /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_02-01-15_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2.1-phase-continuation-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking\n> vitest run\n\n\n RUN  v1.6.1 /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_02-01-15_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2.1-phase-continuation-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking\n\n ✓ src/policy.spec.ts  (21 tests) 4ms\n ✓ src/quote.spec.ts  (23 tests) 3ms\n ↓ src/scenario.spec.ts  (15 tests | 15 skipped)\n\n Test Files  2 passed | 1 skipped (3)\n      Tests  44 passed | 15 todo (59)\n   Start at  05:20:23\n   Duration  421ms (transform 47ms, setup 0ms, collect 55ms, tests 7ms, environment 0ms, prepare 133ms)\n\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784956824098},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"All 21 tests pass. Now let me do my refactoring analysis.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"All 21 policy tests are green. Now let me analyze the code for refactoring opportunities.\n\n## Step 1: Naming Evaluation\n\n**Function-by-function naming review:**\n\n| Function | Current Name | Assessment |\n|---|---|---|\n| `createPolicy` | ✓ Clear | Purpose evident |\n| `processClaim` | ✓ Clear | Purpose evident |\n| `validateDamage` | ⚠️ Misleading | Does TWO things: looks up the policy item AND validates the amount is non-negative. Returns the item too — name doesn't say that. |\n| `computeDamagePayout` | ✓ Clear | Returns gross − deductible (pre-floor, pre-cap) |\n| `countBy` | ✓ Clear | Generic helper |\n| `validateDamageCounts` | ✓ Clear | Validates count-based damage limits |\n\n**Magic numbers:**\n- `8` (in `computeDamagePayout`) — the high-enchantment threshold, unexplained.\n- `0.5` (in `computeDamagePayout`) — the high-enchantment reimbursement factor, unexplained.\n\nThese contrast with `quote.ts`, where every threshold and rate is a named constant with a docstring. Style inconsistency hurts Rule 2.\n\n## Step 2: Initial APP Mass\n\n```\nConstants:  100 (DEDUCT), 1000, 600, 800, 400, 250, 250, 2 (cap×), 0 (??), 8 (threshold),\n            0.5 (factor), 0 (totalRaw), 0 (?? 0), 0 (?? 0) = 14\nBindings:   ~22 (DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE, INSURANCE_VALUES, items, insuranceSum, cap,\n             sum, item, policy, damage, item, isHighEnchant, gross, items, keyFn,\n             counts, item, key, damages, damageCounts, policyCounts, available,\n             totalRaw, payout)\n            ≈ 22\nInvocations: ~16 (reduce+, items.find×2, countBy×2, Map.set/get, validateDamageCounts,\n             validateDamage, computeDamagePayout, +/-, Math.min/floor) = 32\nConditionals: 2 (in validateDamage) + 1 (ternary in computeDamagePayout) = 12\nLoops:      1 (reduce) + 1 (find) + 1 (for in countBy) + 1 (for in validateDamageCounts)\n            + 1 (for in processClaim) = 25\nAssignments: ~5 (sum=0, cap=, insuranceSum=, count set, totalRaw=0, totalRaw+=)\n            ≈ 30\n\nTotal Mass ≈ 135\n```\n\n## Step 3: Apply Simple Design Rules\n\n**Rule 1 (Tests Pass):** ✓ All 21 green.\n\n**Rule 2 (Reveals Intent):**\n- `validateDamage` returns a value but its name says \"validate\". Splitting clarifies responsibility.\n- The `8` and `0.5` are bare numbers; named constants self-document.\n\n**Rule 3 (No Duplication):** No obvious duplication. The `counts.get(key) ?? 0` pattern is idiomatic and twice is the natural repetition for a Map counter.\n\n**Rule 4 (Fewest Elements):** Adding two constants adds elements, but they serve clear purpose (Rule 2). Splitting one function into two adds an element (extra function call), but each now has a single clear job (Rule 2).\n\n## Step 4: Refactoring Decision\n\nI'll make **two coordinated Rule 2 improvements**:\n\n1. **Extract magic numbers `8` and `0.5`** as named constants with docstrings (matches the style of `quote.ts`).\n2. **Split `validateDamage`** into single-responsibility functions: `findPolicyItem` (lookup) and `validateDamageAmount` (validation).\n\nThese both elevate Rule 2 — clarity trumps the marginal mass increase."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_a911008abe3045eb8bcdc0cb","name":"write","arguments":{"content":"/**\n * Per-event deductible subtracted from every damage payout (in G).\n */\nconst DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE = 100;\n\n/**\n * Enchantment level at which the \"high-enchantment\" clause kicks in for damage\n * claims. Per spec, items enchanted at or above this level receive only a\n * fraction of the claimed damage before the deductible is applied.\n */\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD = 8;\n\n/**\n * Multiplier applied to the damage amount for items meeting the\n * high-enchantment threshold. Per spec: such items are reimbursed at 50 %.\n */\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_FACTOR = 0.5;\n\n/**\n * Insurance values (in G) per item type. Per spec:\n *   sword=1000, amulet=600, staff=800, potion=400;\n *   components (rune/moonstone)=250 each.\n */\nconst INSURANCE_VALUES: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 1000,\n  amulet: 600,\n  staff: 800,\n  potion: 400,\n  rune: 250,\n  moonstone: 250,\n};\n\nexport type PolicyItem = {\n  type: string;\n  material?: string;\n  enchantment?: number;\n  cursed?: boolean;\n};\n\nexport type Policy = {\n  items: PolicyItem[];\n  insuranceSum: number;\n  cap: number;\n  remainingCap: number;\n};\n\nexport type Damage = { itemType: string; amount: number };\n\nexport type Incident = { cause: string; damages: Damage[] };\n\nexport function createPolicy(items: PolicyItem[]): Policy {\n  const insuranceSum = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + INSURANCE_VALUES[item.type], 0);\n  const cap = insuranceSum * 2;\n  return { items, insuranceSum, cap, remainingCap: cap };\n}\n\n/**\n * Look up the policy item corresponding to a damage entry. Throws when the\n * damaged item type is not part of the policy.\n */\nfunction findPolicyItem(policy: Policy, damage: Damage): PolicyItem {\n  const item = policy.items.find((i) => i.type === damage.itemType);\n  if (!item) {\n    throw new Error(`Damage references item type not in policy: ${damage.itemType}`);\n  }\n  return item;\n}\n\n/**\n * Ensure a damage entry reports a non-negative amount.\n */\nfunction validateDamageAmount(damage: Damage): void {\n  if (damage.amount < 0) {\n    throw new Error(`Damage amount cannot be negative: ${damage.amount}`);\n  }\n}\n\n/**\n * Compute the raw payout for a single damage event, before flooring and the\n * per-claim cap are applied. Applies the high-enchantment clause (multiplies\n * the damage by HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_FACTOR) when the item is enchanted at or\n * above HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD, then subtracts the per-event deductible.\n */\nfunction computeDamagePayout(item: PolicyItem, damage: Damage): number {\n  const isHighEnchant = (item.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD;\n  const gross = isHighEnchant ? damage.amount * HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_FACTOR : damage.amount;\n  return gross - DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE;\n}\n\nfunction countBy<T>(items: readonly T[], keyFn: (item: T) => string): Map<string, number> {\n  const counts = new Map<string, number>();\n  for (const item of items) {\n    const key = keyFn(item);\n    counts.set(key, (counts.get(key) ?? 0) + 1);\n  }\n  return counts;\n}\n\nfunction validateDamageCounts(policy: Policy, damages: readonly Damage[]): void {\n  const damageCounts = countBy(damages, (d) => d.itemType);\n  const policyCounts = countBy(policy.items, (i) => i.type);\n  for (const [type, count] of damageCounts) {\n    const available = policyCounts.get(type) ?? 0;\n    if (count > available) {\n      throw new Error(\n        `More damages of type \"${type}\" (${count}) than items in policy (${available})`,\n      );\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n/**\n * Process a claim against a policy:\n *   1. Verify no damage type is over-counted vs the items in the policy.\n *   2. Sum the per-damage raw payouts (high-enchant clause applied,\n *      per-event deductible subtracted per damage).\n *   3. Floor the sum and clamp it to the policy's remaining cap.\n */\nexport function processClaim(\n  policy: Policy,\n  incident: Incident,\n): { payout: number; remainingCap: number } {\n  validateDamageCounts(policy, incident.damages);\n  let totalRaw = 0;\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {\n    const item = findPolicyItem(policy, damage);\n    validateDamageAmount(damage);\n    totalRaw += computeDamagePayout(item, damage);\n  }\n  const payout = Math.min(Math.floor(totalRaw), policy.remainingCap);\n  return { payout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout };\n}\n","path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_02-01-15_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2.1-phase-continuation-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/src/policy.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/minimax-m3","usage":{"input":274,"output":2010,"cacheRead":20352,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":17,"totalTokens":22636,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784956824099,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-7c7e94eb-bc0b-498f-85aa-16de04b1cd71","responseModel":"minimax/minimax-m3"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_a911008abe3045eb8bcdc0cb","toolName":"write","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully wrote 4214 bytes to /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_02-01-15_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2.1-phase-continuation-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/src/policy.ts"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784956860148},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_d08cd0ecc35b4cbeb3bed972","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"pnpm test 2>&1 | tail -15"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/minimax-m3","usage":{"input":180,"output":37,"cacheRead":22528,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1,"totalTokens":22745,"cost":{"