t`, `ClaimResult`, `StepResult`\n\nThe type definitions themselves don't count in APP mass (APP measures runtime elements in the function body). But fewer types = fewer elements = Rule 4 win.\n\nFor the function `runScenario` after refactoring:\n```typescript\nexport function runScenario(scenario: Scenario): { results: StepResult[] } {\n  const results: StepResult[] = [];\n  const policies: Policy[] = [];\n  for (const step of scenario.steps) {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      const isFollowup = policies.length > 0;\n      const premium = quotePremium(step.items, scenario.customer, { isFollowup });\n      policies.push(createPolicy(step.items));\n      results.push({ premium });\n    } else {\n      const result = applyClaim(policies[step.policy], step.incident);\n      results.push(result);\n    }\n  }\n  return { results };\n}\n```\n\nComponents:\n- Constants: 0 = 0\n- Bindings: \n  - `scenario` (param) = 1\n  - `results` = 1\n  - `policies` = 1\n  - `step` = 1\n  - `isFollowup` = 1\n  - `premium` = 1\n  - `result` = 1\n  - Total = 7\n- Invocations:\n  - `policies.length` (in `>0`) = 1\n  - `quotePremium(...)` = 1\n  - `policies.push(...)` = 1\n  - `createPolicy(...)` = 1\n  - `results.push(...)` (in quote branch) = 1\n  - `applyClaim(...)` = 1\n  - `results.push(...)` (in claim branch) = 1\n  - Total = 7 * 2 = 14\n- Conditionals: 1 = 4\n- Loops: 1 = 5\n- Assignments: 0 = 0\n\nTotal Mass = 0 + 7 + 14 + 4 + 5 + 0 = 30\n\nBefore refactoring: 35\nAfter refactoring: 30\nMass Change: 35 → 30 (delta -5, or 14% reduction)\n\nWait, but I added a new helper `applyClaim`. Let me count its mass too:\n\n```typescript\nfunction applyClaim(policy: Policy, incident: Incident): ClaimResult {\n  const result = processClaim(policy, incident);\n  policy.remainingCap = result.remainingCap;\n  return result;\n}\n```\n\n- Constants: 0\n- Bindings: 3 (policy, incident, result) = 3\n- Invocations: 1 (processClaim) = 2\n- Conditionals: 0\n- Loops: 0\n- Assignments: 1 (policy.remainingCap = ...) = 6\n\nMass = 0 + 3 + 2 + 0 + 0 + 6 = 11\n\nTotal project mass (runScenario + applyClaim):\nBefore: 35\nAfter: 30 + 11 = 41\n\nHmm, that's actually MORE mass. But the rule is that Rule 2 trumps APP. Let me think about whether clarity improved.\n\nBefore:\n```typescript\nconst result = processClaim(policies[step.policy], step.incident);\npolicies[step.policy].remainingCap = result.remainingCap;\nresults.push(result);\n```\n\nAfter:\n```typescript\nconst result = applyClaim(policies[step.policy], step.incident);\nresults.push(result);\n```\n\nThe after version is clearer:\n1. We don't see the mutation - it's hidden in applyClaim\n2. The intent \"apply a claim\" is named\n3. There's a comment in applyClaim explaining why we mutate\n\nBut the mass is higher. Let me reconsider.\n\nActually, looking at this more carefully - the original code is also fairly clear. The mutation is a side effect of processing claims (you process them, then update the cap). It's not too surprising.\n\nMaybe I should:\n1. Skip the `applyClaim` extraction (keep mass lower)\n2. Just add a comment explaining the mutation\n3. Still remove the type duplications\n\nThat keeps the code clear while reducing mass. Let me think...\n\nActually, let me reconsider Rule 4: \"Fewest Elements - Lowest priority - minimize code elements\". The `applyClaim` adds one element. The current code has 2 lines that could be combined or commented.\n\nAlternative: Add a comment in runScenario and keep the mutation inline:\n```typescript\n} else {\n  // processClaim returns the new cap; persist it back to the policy so that\n  // subsequent claims against the same policy see the updated cap.\n  const result = processClaim(policies[step.policy], step.incident);\n  policies[step.policy].remainingCap = result.remainingCap;\n  results.push(result);\n}\n```\n\nThis adds no new elements, just clarifies intent.\n\nHmm. Both approaches have merit. Let me think about what makes the code better:\n\nOption A: Extract `applyClaim` (adds mass, removes duplication of policy mutation logic, names the intent)\nOption B: Add a comment in place (no mass change, but the mutation is still inline)\n\nLooking at the current code, the pattern \"processClaim → assign remainingCap → push result\" is a single conceptual operation. The fact that it's split across 3 lines is fine. Adding a comment makes the mutation's purpose clear.\n\nActually, I think a cleaner approach: keep the code as-is, add a brief comment explaining why we mutate. The current code is already reasonably clean.\n\nBut wait - is there ANY duplication in scenario.ts that's worth extracting? Let me look:\n- The `policies.push(...)` pattern appears twice\n- The `results.push(...)` pattern appears twice\n- The `policies[step.policy]` indexing appears twice\n\nNone of these are meaningful duplications - they're just common JS patterns.\n\nLet me think about a different approach. What if I extract two clear helpers?\n- `handleQuoteStep` \n- `handleClaimStep`\n\n```typescript\nfunction handleQuoteStep(step: QuoteStep, customer: Customer, policies: Policy[], results: StepResult[]): void {\n  const isFollowup = policies.length > 0;\n  const premium = quotePremium(step.items, customer, { isFollowup });\n  policies.push(createPolicy(step.items));\n  results.push({ premium });\n}\n\nfunction handleClaimStep(step: ClaimStep, policies: Policy[], results: StepResult[]): void {\n  const result = processClaim(policies[step.policy], step.incident);\n  policies[step.policy].remainingCap = result.remainingCap;\n  results.push(result);\n}\n\nexport function runScenario(scenario: Scenario): { results: StepResult[] } {\n  const results: StepResult[] = [];\n  const policies: Policy[] = [];\n  for (const step of scenario.steps) {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      handleQuoteStep(step, scenario.customer, policies, results);\n    } else {\n      handleClaimStep(step, policies, results);\n    }\n  }\n  return { results };\n}\n```\n\nThis:\n- Names each step type's handling\n- Removes inline complexity from the loop body\n- Makes the loop body a clean dispatch\n\nMass of helpers:\n- handleQuoteStep:\n  - Bindings: step, customer, policies, results, isFollowup, premium = 6\n  - Invocations: length, quotePremium, push, createPolicy, push = 5 * 2 = 10\n  - Assignments: 0\n  - Mass = 16\n\n- handleClaimStep:\n  - Bindings: step, policies, results, result = 4\n  - Invocations: processClaim, push = 2 * 2 = 4\n  - Assignments: 1 = 6\n  - Mass = 14\n\nMass of runScenario (refactored):\n- Bindings: scenario, results, policies, step = 4\n- Invocations: 0 (no direct calls, just dispatch)\n- Conditionals: 1 = 4\n- Loops: 1 = 5\n- Assignments: 0\n- Mass = 13\n\nTotal: 16 + 14 + 13 = 43\n\nBefore: 35\nAfter: 43\n\nThat's worse on APP. And I added 3 helpers. Rule 4 says fewer elements.\n\nLet me reconsider. The original code is:\n- 1 function (runScenario)\n- Clear two-branch structure\n\nI think extracting helpers here is over-engineering. The original structure is already clear.\n\nOK so my refactoring plan:\n1. Remove `ScenarioCustomer` - use `Customer` from quote.ts (DRY)\n2. Use `Incident` type for `ClaimStep.incident` instead of inline structure (DRY)\n3. Add a brief comment explaining the mutation in the claim branch (clarity)\n\nThis minimizes mass changes while improving clarity and removing duplication.\n\nLet me also consider: should I keep the structure of `runScenario` mostly the same?\n\nFinal refactored code:\n\n```typescript\nimport { createPolicy, processClaim, type Policy, type Incident } from \"./policy.js\";\nimport { quotePremium, type QuoteItem, type Customer } from \"./quote.js\";\n\nexport type QuoteStep = {\n  op: \"quote\";\n  items: QuoteItem[];\n};\n\nexport type ClaimStep = {\n  op: \"claim\";\n  policy: number;\n  incident: Incident;\n};\n\nexport type Step = QuoteStep | ClaimStep;\n\nexport type Scenario = {\n  customer: Customer;\n  steps: Step[];\n};\n\nexport type QuoteResult = { premium: number };\nexport type ClaimResult = { payout: number; remainingCap: number };\nexport type StepResult = QuoteResult | ClaimResult;\n\nexport function runScenario(scenario: Scenario): { results: StepResult[] } {\n  const results: StepResult[] = [];\n  const policies: Policy[] = [];\n  for (const step of scenario.steps) {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      const isFollowup = policies.length > 0;\n      const premium = quotePremium(step.items, scenario.customer, { isFollowup });\n      policies.push(createPolicy(step.items));\n      results.push({ premium });\n    } else {\n      // processClaim returns the new cap as a value; persist it on the policy\n      // so subsequent claims against the same policy see the updated cap.\n      const result = processClaim(policies[step.policy], step.incident);\n      policies[step.policy].remainingCap = result.remainingCap;\n      results.push(result);\n    }\n  }\n  return { results };\n}\n```\n\nLet me calculate the new mass:\n\nConstants: 0\nBindings:\n- scenario = 1\n- results = 1\n- policies = 1\n- step = 1\n- isFollowup = 1\n- premium = 1\n- result = 1\n= 7\n\nInvocations:\n- policies.length = 1\n- quotePremium = 1\n- policies.push = 1\n- createPolicy = 1\n- results.push (quote branch) = 1\n- processClaim = 1\n- results.push (claim branch) = 1\n= 7 * 2 = 14\n\nConditionals: 1 = 4\nLoops: 1 = 5\nAssignments: 1 (remainingCap = ...) = 6\n\nTotal Mass = 0 + 7 + 14 + 4 + 5 + 6 = 36\n\nHmm, that's 1 MORE than the original 35. But wait, I removed `ScenarioCustomer` and inlined the incident type. Those don't directly affect the function body's mass.\n\nActually, comparing:\n- Before: `quotePremium(step.items, scenario.customer, { isFollowup })` - same\n- Before: `processClaim(policies[step.policy], step.incident)` - same\n\nThe function body is identical except for the comment (which doesn't count in APP). The \"mass\" is functionally the same.\n\nSo before = 35, after = 35 (function body). Plus removed type definitions (Rule 4 win) and replaced inline types with imports (Rule 3 win).\n\nWait, let me recount the original:\n\nOriginal:\n```typescript\nexport function runScenario(scenario: Scenario): { results: StepResult[] } {\n  const results: StepResult[] = [];\n  const policies: Policy[] = [];\n  for (const step of scenario.steps) {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      const isFollowup = policies.length > 0;\n      const premium = quotePremium(step.items, scenario.customer, { isFollowup });\n      policies.push(createPolicy(step.items));\n      results.push({ premium });\n    } else {\n      const result = processClaim(policies[step.policy], step.incident);\n      policies[step.policy].remainingCap = result.remainingCap;\n      results.push(result);\n    }\n  }\n  return { results };\n}\n```\n\nComponents:\n- Constants: 1 (the `0` in `policies.length > 0`) = 1\n- Bindings: 7 (same as above) = 7\n- Invocations: 7 = 14\n- Conditionals: 1 = 4\n- Loops: 1 = 5\n- Assignments: 1 = 6\n\nTotal = 1 + 7 + 14 + 4 + 5 + 6 = 37\n\nHmm, let me recount. The `0` in `policies.length > 0` - is that a constant? Yes, literals like `0`, `1`, etc. count as constants. So 1.\n\nSo original = 37, refactored = 36. Same mass, basically. The improvement is purely in clarity and DRY.\n\nActually, I realize my counting has been inconsistent. Let me think about what's a constant:\n- `0` in `policies.length > 0` - yes, it's a literal\n- But that's a comparison value, used once\n\nOK let me just say: function body mass is approximately the same. The benefit is in:\n1. Fewer types defined (Rule 4)\n2. Removed duplication (Rule 3)\n3. Comment explains the non-obvious mutation (Rule 2)\n\nLet me also check that I haven't missed anything in the simplification.\n\nActually, I want to double-check `Incident` from policy.ts:\n- `export type Incident = { cause: string; damages: Damage[] };`\n- `export type Damage = { itemType: string; amount: number };`\n\nSo `Incident = { cause: string; damages: { itemType: string; amount: number }[] }` - identical to the inline type. Good.\n\nNow let me verify that this won't break tests. Let me look at the test file:\n```typescript\n{ op: \"claim\", policy: 0, incident: { cause: \"fire\", damages: [{ itemType: \"amulet\", amount: 200 }] } }\n```\n\nThis works with `Incident` type because the inline structure matches.\n\nOK, let me proceed with the refactor.\n\nLet me also think about whether to make a more impactful refactoring. Looking again at the code:\n\n```typescript\nif (step.op === \"quote\") {\n  ...\n} else {\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nCould this be a switch? Or could I dispatch via a Map? For just two cases, the if/else is fine. Adding a switch would be over-engineering.\n\nCould I use polymorphism with a `step.process()` method on each step type? That would be over-engineering for this small scenario.\n\nI think my current refactoring plan is good:\n1. Remove `ScenarioCustomer` (use `Customer` from quote.ts)\n2. Use `Incident` type for `ClaimStep.incident` instead of inline structure\n3. Add brief comment explaining the mutation\n\nLet me make these changes.\n\nWait, one more thought: I could also rename `policies` to something more descriptive, but `policies` is fine - it's a list of policies indexed by creation order.\n\nAnd `results` is fine - it's a list of step results.\n\nLet me proceed.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Let me run the tests to confirm baseline and then proceed with refactoring."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_3da18e78fb3f459c8e080b5d","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cat /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_02-01-15_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2.1-phase-continuation-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/package.json"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/minimax-m3","usage":{"input":361,"output":7315,"cacheRead":10624,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":7226,"totalTokens":18300,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason"