*\n\n1. The else branch has an unclear pattern - we get `result` from `processClaim` and then mutate `policies[step.policy].remainingCap = result.remainingCap`. This is a side-effect-heavy pattern.\n\n2. There's an asymmetry between quote and claim:\n   - Quote: build it inline, push to results\n   - Claim: call helper, mutate policy, push to results\n   \n   We could extract a `runQuoteStep` helper and `runClaimStep` helper to make the structure more symmetric and clear.\n\n3. The `if/else` based on `step.op` could be replaced with a more declarative pattern. But TypeScript discriminated unions + switch is fine and idiomatic.\n\n4. The mutation `policies[step.policy].remainingCap = result.remainingCap` - this is required to keep the policy state across steps. We could make this more explicit by reassigning: `policies[step.policy] = { ...policies[step.policy], remainingCap: result.remainingCap }` but the mutation pattern works fine and is clearer.\n\n5. The comment \"processClaim returns the new cap as a value; persist it on the policy so subsequent claims against the same policy see the updated cap.\" is a useful comment that explains intent.\n\n**Possible refactorings:**\n\nLet me think about Rule 2 (Reveals Intent):\n- The current code is fairly compact. Extracting helpers might add complexity for little gain.\n- One thing I notice is that the if/else could become a switch statement on `step.op` for better discriminated union narrowing, but `if (step.op === \"quote\")` already narrows well.\n\nLet me think about Rule 3 (No Duplication):\n- Not much duplication in this small file.\n\nLet me think about Rule 4 (Fewest Elements):\n- The function is already quite minimal.\n\nHmm, the code is already quite clean. But there might be a small improvement:\n\n**Option A: Extract helper functions for clarity**\n```typescript\nfunction runQuoteStep(step: QuoteStep, customer: Customer, existingPolicies: Policy[]): { result: QuoteResult; newPolicy: Policy } {\n  const isFollowup = existingPolicies.length > 0;\n  const premium = quotePremium(step.items, customer, { isFollowup });\n  return {\n    result: { premium },\n    newPolicy: createPolicy(step.items),\n  };\n}\n\nfunction runClaimStep(step: ClaimStep, policies: Policy[]): ClaimResult {\n  const result = processClaim(policies[step.policy], step.incident);\n  policies[step.policy].remainingCap = result.remainingCap;\n  return result;\n}\n```\n\nThis would make the main loop super clear. But this adds two more functions (mass increases), and the current version is already compact.\n\n**Option B: Use switch statement on `step.op` for clearer discriminated union pattern**\n\n```typescript\nfor (const step of scenario.steps) {\n  switch (step.op) {\n    case \"quote\": {\n      const isFollowup = policies.length > 0;\n      const premium = quotePremium(step.items, scenario.customer, { isFollowup });\n      policies.push(createPolicy(step.items));\n      results.push({ premium });\n      break;\n    }\n    case \"claim\": {\n      const result = processClaim(policies[step.policy], step.incident);\n      policies[step.policy].remainingCap = result.remainingCap;\n      results.push(result);\n      break;\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis is slightly heavier on mass (extra block statement).\n\n**Option C: Use a switch-expression-style with `continue` (TypeScript)**\nActually we can use a ternary but it would be less clear.\n\n**Option D: Extract a `runStep` helper function that handles a single step**\n\nThis is the cleanest improvement, as it:\n- Reduces the main function to a clean loop\n- Each branch's logic is isolated in its own helper\n- Makes the overall flow obvious\n\nLet me think more carefully. The current structure is:\n\n```typescript\nexport function runScenario(scenario: Scenario): { results: StepResult[] } {\n  const results: StepResult[] = [];\n  const policies: Policy[] = [];\n  for (const step of scenario.steps) {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      // 3 statements\n    } else {\n      // 3 statements\n    }\n  }\n  return { results };\n}\n```\n\nThe if/else with the discriminated union pattern is actually quite clean and idiomatic in TypeScript. Extracting helpers would add mass and might not improve clarity significantly.\n\n**The cleanest improvement would be Option D**: extract per-step helpers. But it's a tradeoff.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The current `runScenario` is:\n- 23 lines of code (including blanks)\n- 6 bindings (`results`, `policies`, `step`, `isFollowup`, `premium`, `result`)\n- 1 loop\n- 2 conditionals (well, 1 if/else which is 1 conditional)\n- 5 invocations (quotePremium, createPolicy, processClaim, .push x2)\n- 4 assignments (results push x2, isFollowup, premium, remainingCap = ...)\n\nWait, let me re-check. Let me actually count the mass:\n\n```typescript\nexport function runScenario(scenario: Scenario): { results: StepResult[] } {\n  const results: StepResult[] = [];                                  // assignment\n  const policies: Policy[] = [];                                      // assignment\n  for (const step of scenario.steps) {                                // loop + binding\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {                                        // conditional + binding\n      const isFollowup = policies.length > 0;                         // binding + assignment + invocation\n      const premium = quotePremium(step.items, scenario.customer, { isFollowup });  // binding + assignment + invocation\n      policies.push(createPolicy(step.items));                        // invocation (push) + invocation (createPolicy)\n      results.push({ premium });                                      // invocation (push) + binding\n    } else {\n      // processClaim returns the new cap as a value; persist it on the policy\n      // so subsequent claims against the same policy see the updated cap.\n      const result = processClaim(policies[step.policy], step.incident);  // binding + assignment + invocation\n    