                                // binding(results) + assignment\n// Line 3\n  const policies: Policy[] = [];                                              // binding(policies) + assignment\n// Line 4\n  for (const step of scenario.steps) {                                        // binding(step) + loop\n// Line 5\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {                                                // conditional\n// Line 6\n      const isFollowup = policies.length > 0;                                 // binding(isFollowup) + assignment + constant(0)\n// Line 7\n      const premium = quotePremium(step.items, scenario.customer, { isFollowup });  // binding(premium) + assignment + 2 invocations\n// Line 8\n      policies.push(createPolicy(step.items));                                // invocation(push) + invocation(createPolicy)\n// Line 9\n      results.push({ premium });                                              // invocation(push) + binding(premium in object)\n// Line 10\n    } else {\n// Line 11\n      // processClaim returns the new cap as a value; persist it on the policy\n// Line 12\n      // so subsequent claims against the same policy see the updated cap.\n// Line 13\n      const result = processClaim(policies[step.policy], step.incident);     // binding(result) + assignment + invocation\n// Line 14\n      policies[step.policy].remainingCap = result.remainingCap;              // assignment\n// Line 15\n      results.push(result);                                                   // invocation(push)\n// Line 16\n    }\n// Line 17\n  }\n// Line 18\n  return { results };                                                         // binding(results) + invocation(return)\n// Line 19\n}\n```\n\nOK, let me just do the count:\n\nConstants (literal values):\n- `\"quote\"` = 1\n- `0` (in `> 0`) = 1\nTotal: 2\n\nBindings (named references):\n- `scenario` (parameter) = 1\n- `results` = 1\n- `policies` = 1\n- `step` (loop var) = 1\n- `isFollowup` = 1\n- `premium` = 1\n- `result` = 1\n- `results` (in return) = 1 (well, it's a destructuring binding)\nTotal: 8\n\nWait, the `{ results }` in the return is a destructuring binding. I'll count it once.\n\nActually, I realize I'm being inconsistent. Let me start over with a clearer methodology.\n\nI'll use the APP mass table:\n- Constants (literal values): 1 each\n- Bindings (named references, including parameters and const declarations): 1 each\n- Invocations (function/method calls): 2 each\n- Conditionals (if/else, ternary, switch): 4 each\n- Loops (for, while, forEach): 5 each\n- Assignments (mutations including const initializations): 6 each\n\nHmm wait, in TDD/Refactoring Kata style I've seen, \"assignments\" are mutations, not declarations. Let me re-read the rule:\n\n> **Assignment** (Mass: 6): Mutations (`x = 5`, `count++`)\n\nSo `const x = ...` is a binding (1) but `x = 5` (re-assignment) is an assignment (6). Let me look again at the rule definition:\n\n> **Total Mass** = (constants x 1) + (bindings x 1) + (invocations x 2) + (conditionals x 4) + (loops x 5) + (assignments x 6)\n\n> **Binding/Scalar** (Mass: 1): Variables, parameters (`amount`, `result`)\n\nHmm, so `const results = []` is a binding (1) + an assignment (6)?\n\nActually wait, I think assignments only count for explicit mutations, not declarations. Let me look at the example:\n\n```\n**Current Code Mass**:\nfunction calculate(numbers: number[]): number {\n  return numbers.reduce((sum, num) => sum + num, 0);\n}\n\nComponent Count:\n- Constants: 1 (literal 0) = 1\n- Bindings: 3 (numbers, sum, num) = 3\n- Invocations: 2 (reduce, +) = 4\n- Conditionals: 0 = 0\n- Loops: 1 (reduce is iteration) = 5\n- Assignments: 0 = 0\n\nTotal Mass: 13\n```\n\nIn this example, `sum + num` doesn't count as an assignment, just an invocation. The `0` in `reduce((sum, num) => sum + num, 0)` counts as a constant. There are no `=` assignments.\n\nI think for `const x = ...`:\n- The `x` is a binding (1)\n- The `=` operator might count as an assignment (6) since it's a state mutation\n\nHmm, but `const` is immutable so it's not really a mutation. But it does assign a value to a name.\n\nActually, looking at the example carefully: `sum` is bound to a closure variable. The closure variable gets assigned every iteration. So `sum = sum + num` would be an assignment. But the example says no assignments.\n\nI think the rule is: an `=` on a `const` declaration is just initialization, not an \"assignment\" in the sense of the APP rule. The APP rule is about mutations of existing state.\n\nBut wait, the example has `sum + num` which doesn't include `=`. And `reduce` uses a callback to update `sum` internally. There's no explicit `=` in the example.\n\nLet me interpret the rule generously:\n- A `const x = expr` declaration introduces a binding (1)\n- It also assigns (6) once at that point\n\nBut then every `const` would add 6 mass. That's probably not the intent.\n\nLet me look at this differently. I think the rule is:\n- \"Assignment\" specifically refers to mutation of an existing variable\n- A `const x = ...` declaration is just a binding (1), not an assignment\n\nThis matches the example - `const x = 5` would just be 1 mass (the binding).\n\nOK, with that interpretation, let me count again:\n\nFor the current `runScenario`:\n\nConstants (1 each):\n- `\"quote\"` = 1\n- `0` (in `policies.length > 0`) = 1\nTotal: 2\n\nBindings (1 each):\n- `scenario` (parameter) = 1\n- `results` (const) = 1\n- `policies` (const) = 1\n- `step` (loop var) = 1\n- `isFollowup` (const) = 1\n- `premium` (const) = 1\n- `result` (const) = 1\n- `results` (in `return { results }` destructuring) = 1\nTotal: 8\n\nInvocations (2 each):\n- `quotePremium(...)` = 2\n- `policies.push(createPolicy(...))` = 2 (push) + 2 (createPolicy) = 4\n- `results.push({ premium })` = 2 (push) = 2\n- `processClaim(...)` = 2\n- `results.push(result)` = 2\nTotal: 10\n\nConditionals (4 each):\n- `if (step.op === \"quote\") { ... } else { ... }` = 4\nTotal: 4\n\nLoops (5 each):\n- `for (const step of scenario.steps)` = 5\nTotal: 5\n\nAssignments (6 each):\n- `policies[step.policy].remainingCap = result.remainingCap` = 6\nTotal: 6\n\nGrand total: 2 + 8 + 10 + 4 + 5 + 6 = 35\n\nHmm, that's a lot. Let me reconsider some of these.\n\nActually, \"object literal\" in `results.push({ premium })` doesn't count as a constant (it's not a literal value being compared or used standalone). It's just an object creation. But maybe it counts as an invocation (object constructor)?\n\nAnd `scenario.steps` is property access, which doesn't fit cleanly into any category.\n\nYou know what, the mass calculation is approximate and I'll just use it as a rough guide. Let me just identify potential improvements without obsessing over exact mass numbers.\n\n**Improvement Candidates:**\n\nLooking at the code again, the most promising refactoring candidates are:\n\n1. **Extract `runQuoteStep` and `runClaimStep` helpers** - This is the clearest improvement. The main loop becomes a clean dispatcher, and each helper is self-contained.\n\n2. **Remove the unnecessary comment about processClaim** - The comment is helpful for explaining intent, but the code is already clear if we extract helpers.\n\n3. **Naming**: \n   - `isFollowup` is clear\n   - `step.policy` is clear (but maybe could be `step.policyIndex`?)\n   - Actually `step.policy` in ClaimStep refers to a policy index, not a policy. This is a bit confusing.\n\nWait, let me check the ClaimStep definition:\n```typescript\nexport type ClaimStep = {\n  op: \"claim\";\n  policy: number;\n  incident: Incident;\n};\n```\n\n`policy: number` is actually the **index** of the policy, not a policy itself. The field is named `policy` but it's used as an index: `policies[step.policy]`. This is misleading naming!\n\nA `ClaimStep.policy` of `0` means \"the first policy\", not a policy. The name should be `policyIndex`.\n\nThis is a clear naming improvement (Rule 2)! Renaming `policy` to `policyIndex` in `ClaimStep` would make the intent clearer.\n\nLet me verify by looking at the test cases:\n- `policy: 0` is used in many tests, where the user means \"the first policy\"\n- The actual usage is `policies[step.policy]` which confirms it's an index\n\nThis is a great refactoring opportunity!\n\nLet me also consider:\n\n**Option: Extract helpers + Rename policy to policyIndex**\n\nThis would be a comprehensive refactoring:\n\n```typescript\nimport { createPolicy, processClaim, type Policy, type Incident } from \"./policy.js\";\nimport { quotePremium, type QuoteItem, type Customer } from \"./quote.js\";\n\nexport type QuoteStep = {\n  op: \"quote\";\n  items: QuoteItem[];\n};\n\nexport type ClaimStep = {\n  op: \"claim\";\n  policyIndex: number;  // renamed from \"policy\"\n  incident: Incident;\n};\n\nexport type Step = QuoteStep | ClaimStep;\n\nexport type Scenario = {\n  customer: Customer;\n  steps: Step[];\n};\n\nexport type QuoteResult = { premium: number };\nexport type ClaimResult = { payout: number; remainingCap: number };\nexport type StepResult = QuoteResult | ClaimResult;\n\nexport function runScenario(scenario: Scenario): { results: StepResult[] } {\n  const results: StepResult[] = [];\n  const policies: Policy[] = [];\n  for (const step of scenario.steps) {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      const premium = quotePremium(step.items, scenario.customer, {\n        isFollowup: policies.length > 0,\n      });\n      policies.push(createPolicy(step.items));\n      results.push({ premium });\n    } else {\n      const result = processClaim(policies[step.policyIndex], step.incident);\n      policies[step.policyIndex].remainingCap = result.remainingCap;\n      results.push(result);\n    }\n  }\n  return { results };\n}\n```\n\nWait, but the tests use `policy: 0` syntax. If I rename the field, the tests will break!\n\nLet me check the test cases again:\n```typescript\n{ op: \"claim\", policy: 0, incident: { ... } }\n```\n\nYes, the tests use `policy: 0`. So renaming the field would break the tests.\n\nHowever, the tests are testing the BEHAVIOR, not the field name. The field name in `ClaimStep` is internal API.\n\nHmm, but the tests construct `ClaimStep` objects directly. So if I rename the field, the tests break.\n\nLooking at the existing passing tests, they use `policy: 0` which is the index. Renaming to `policyIndex` would require updating the tests too.\n\nThis is a tradeoff. If I rename, I need to update tests, but the tests verify behavior not naming.\n\nActually, the agent instructions say \"Never break passing tests\". So I need to be careful. Let me think about this differently.\n\nI have two options:\n1. Rename `policy` → `policyIndex` in the type AND update the tests (acceptable if tests still verify the same behavior)\n2. Keep the field name `policy` and just add a comment explaining it's an index\n\nOption 1 is better because it improves clarity at all levels. The tests can be updated to use `policyIndex: 0` instead of `policy: 0`, which is a one-line change per test.\n\nWait, let me re-read the rule about tests:\n> **Tests must stay green**: Never break passing tests\n\n\"Never break\" means they need to PASS after the refactor. It doesn't mean I can't update the test code. As long as they pass, it's fine.\n\nBut the agent instructions also say:\n> NEVER STOP AT A PHASE BOUNDARY\n\nWe're in the refactor phase. The subagent's job is to refactor. If tests are updated, that's part of the refactoring.\n\nOK, let me think about this more carefully. The refactoring pass is meant to improve the implementation. Updating test field names to match a clearer naming convention is a legitimate refactoring improvement.\n\nActually, looking at the test more carefully:\n\n```typescript\n{ op: \"claim\", policy: 0, incident: { cause: \"fire\", damages: [...] } }\n```\n\nThe `policy: 0` here means \"policy index 0\". The naming is technically misleading because `policy` sounds like it would be a Policy object, not an index.\n\nRenaming to `policyIndex` is a legitimate refactoring improvement.\n\nLet me make this change. Here's my plan:\n\n1. Rename `ClaimStep.policy` → `ClaimStep.policyIndex` in scenario.ts\n2. Update all 5 test cases that use `policy: 0` → `policyIndex: 0`\n3. Update the reference in `runScenario` to use `step.policyIndex`\n4. Run tests to verify\n\nWait, but there are also TODO tests that haven't been activated yet:\n- `it.todo(\"error: damage itemType not in policy → throws Error, no results\");` - this might use the ClaimStep format\n\nLet me check the TODO tests... Looking at the spec file:\n- `it.todo(\"error: damage itemType not in policy → throws Error, no results\");` - this doesn't construct a ClaimStep, just tests error behavior\n- Other TODOs don't construct ClaimStep either\n\nSo only the 4 active tests with `policy: 0` need updating. Let me verify by searching.\n\nActually, I should also consider whether extracting helpers would improve the code. Let me think:\n\nPros of extracting helpers:\n- Makes each branch self-contained\n- Easier to test each branch independently (though we'd need to export them)\n- Each helper has a clear, single purpose\n\nCons of extracting helpers:\n- Adds 2 more function declarations (mass increases)\n- The current code is already small enough that inlining is fine\n- Naming the helpers might be tricky\n\nActually, looking at the code more, I think the current structure is fine. The conditional branching is clear, the names are clear (except for `policy` vs `policyIndex`), and there's no duplication.\n\nThe main improvement is the naming of `policy` → `policyIndex`.\n\nLet me also think about whether to extract helpers. The code is essentially:\n\n```\nfor step in steps:\n  if step is quote:\n    premium = quotePremium(...)\n    create policy and push\n    push result\n  else:  # claim\n    result = processClaim(...)\n    update policy cap\n    push result\n```\n\nIf I extract helpers:\n\n```typescript\nfunction runQuoteStep(step: QuoteStep, customer: Customer, hasPriorPolicies: boolean): { result: QuoteResult; policy: Policy } {\n  const premium = quotePremium(step.items, customer, { isFollowup: hasPriorPolicies });\n  return {\n    result: { premium },\n    policy: createPolicy(step.items),\n  };\n}\n\nfunction runClaimStep(step: ClaimStep, policies: Policy[]): ClaimResult {\n  const result = processClaim(policies[step.policyIndex], step.incident);\n  policies[step.policyIndex].remainingCap = result.remainingCap;\n  return result;\n}\n\nexport function runScenario(scenario: Scenario): { results: StepResult[] } {\n  const results: StepResult[] = [];\n  const policies: Policy[] = [];\n  for (const step of scenario.steps) {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      const { result, policy } = runQuoteStep(step, scenario.customer, policies.length > 0);\n      policies.push(policy);\n      results.push(result);\n    } else {\n      results.push(runClaimStep(step, policies));\n    }\n  }\n  return { results };\n}\n```\n\nHmm, this adds quite a bit of complexity. The `runQuoteStep` helper returns both a result and a policy, which is awkward.\n\nAlternative: extract only the claim step (since it has the side effect mutation):\n\n```typescript\nfunction processClaimStep(policy: Policy, incident: Incident): ClaimResult {\n  const result = processClaim(policy, incident);\n  policy.remainingCap = result.remainingCap;\n  return result;\n}\n```\n\nBut this is essentially just wrapping `processClaim` with the mutation. The wrapper is minor.\n\nI think the cleanest refactoring is:\n1. Rename `policy` → `policyIndex` in ClaimStep (naming improvement)\n2. Update tests to match\n3. Leave the function body as-is (it's already clean)\n\nLet me also check if there's anything else worth improving.\n\nLooking at the import:\n```typescript\nimport { createPolicy, processClaim, type Policy, type Incident } from \"./policy.js\";\nimport { quotePremium, type QuoteItem, type Customer } from \"./quote.js\";\n```\n\nThese imports are clean. `Incident` is only used in the type definition of `ClaimStep`. `Policy` is used in the policies array. Both are necessary.\n\n`QuoteItem` is used in `QuoteStep`. `Customer` is used in `Scenario`. Both necessary.\n\n`createPolicy` is used in `runScenario`. `processClaim` is used in `runScenario`. `quotePremium` is used in `runScenario`. All necessary.\n\nOK, I think the only meaningful refactoring is the naming change. Let me do that.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider the extraction approach one more time. Looking at the code:\n\n```typescript\nif (step.op === \"quote\") {\n  const isFollowup = policies.length > 0;\n  const premium = quotePremium(step.items, scenario.customer, { isFollowup });\n  policies.push(createPolicy(step.items));\n  results.push({ premium });\n} else {\n  const result = processClaim(policies[step.policy], step.incident);\n  policies[step.policy].remainingCap = result.remainingCap;\n  results.push(result);\n}\n```\n\nThe asymmetry between quote and claim is:\n- Quote branch: 4 statements (isFollowup, premium, push policy, push result)\n- Claim branch: 3 statements (result, mutate, push result)\n\nIf I extract helpers, I could make them symmetric. But this is more about aesthetic symmetry than actual clarity gain.\n\nActually, you know what, let me just focus on the naming improvement. It's the most impactful change.\n\n**Plan:**\n1. Rename `ClaimStep.policy` → `ClaimStep.policyIndex`\n2. Update the use site in `runScenario`: `policies[step.policy]` → `policies[step.policyIndex]`\n3. Update all 4 test cases that construct ClaimStep\n4. Run tests to verify\n\nLet me do this now.\n\nWait, I should also evaluate if the `else` branch is necessary. With a discriminated union, we could use a switch or early returns. But if/else is idiomatic.\n\nActually, with discriminated unions, an alternative is:\n\n```typescript\nfor (const step of scenario.steps) {\n  if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n    // ...\n    continue;\n  }\n  // claim branch\n  // ...\n}\n```\n\nBut this is just a stylistic preference.\n\nOK let me proceed with the naming refactoring.\n\nActually, on second thought, let me think about whether `policy` vs `policyIndex` is actually better. \n\nLooking at how it's used:\n- `policies[step.policy]` - the value at this index is a `Policy`\n- The JSON shape has `policy: 0`, which means \"policy #0\" or \"the first policy\"\n\nThe field name `policy` could be interpreted as:\n1. The policy itself (wrong - it's an index)\n2. An identifier for a policy (could work, but ambiguous)\n3. The position of a policy (clear, but `policyIndex` is more explicit)\n\nGiven that we use it as `policies[step.policy]`, it's clear in context. But to a new reader, `policy: 0` is ambiguous - it could be \"the zero policy\", \"policy #0\", or just \"0\".\n\n`policyIndex: 0` is unambiguously \"the index 0 into the policies array\".\n\nYes, renaming to `policyIndex` is a clear improvement.\n\nLet me do it.\n\nActually, I realize I should also check whether there's a `StepResult` vs `QuoteResult | ClaimResult` naming question. They're the same thing - `StepResult` is just a union type alias. That's fine and clear.\n\nOK let me proceed.\n\nOne more thought: the comment on the claim branch says:\n```\n// processClaim returns the new cap as a value; persist it on the policy\n// so subsequent claims against the same policy see the updated cap.\n```\n\nThis is good context but verbose. The code is already clear if you know the policy state needs to be persisted. But the comment is helpful for future readers.\n\nI'll keep this comment as it's informative.\n\nLet me proceed with the refactoring.\n\nActually one more thought: with the rename, the tests need updating too. Let me make sure the change in tests doesn't break the structure. The tests use:\n\n```typescript\n{ op: \"claim\", policy: 0, incident: { cause: \"fire\", damages: [...] } }\n```\n\nAfter rename:\n```typescript\n{ op: \"claim\", policyIndex: 0, incident: { cause: \"fire\", d