lpers**: The branches are short (3-4 statements each), and the dispatch on discriminated union is idiomatic. Extracting helpers would add ~5 mass per helper while not significantly improving clarity. **Rejected — would increase mass without clarity gain.**\n\n2. **Use switch statement instead of if/else**: Pure stylistic change, adds mass (block delimiters). **Rejected — no clarity gain.**\n\n3. **Remove `isFollowup` named const**: Inlining `policies.length > 0` directly into the options object would save one binding but lose the labeling. **Rejected — the named const adds clarity.**\n\n4. **Replace `policies[idx].remainingCap = ...` mutation with `Object.assign` or spread**: Would add mass and lose clarity. **Rejected — current mutation pattern is concise.**\n\n5. **Remove the comment on the claim branch**: It's useful context for why we mutate after the call. **Rejected — keep informative comments.**\n\nThe single high-leverage change was the naming. Field rename satisfies Rule 2 and clarifies the JSON shape at all boundaries (tests, type, use sites).","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"## Refactoring Complete\n\n**Refactoring**: Renamed `ClaimStep.policy` → `ClaimStep.policyIndex` (and updated all 5 use sites in tests + 2 in implementation) to reveal that the field holds an **index** into the `policies[]` array, not a Policy reference. Added a doc comment explaining the 0-based, quote-order semantics.\n\n**Mass Change**: 35 → 35 (no change in component count — pure rename)\n\n| Rule | Evaluation |\n|---|---|\n| **Rule 1: Tests Pass** | ✅ All 51 tests still passing (7 active scenario tests + 44 other tests) |\n| **Rule 2: Reveals Intent** | ✅ Improved — `policyIndex: 0` is unambiguous; `policy: 0` read like a Policy object |\n| **Rule 3: No Duplication** | ✅ No duplication in the file; nothing to extract |\n| **Rule 4: Fewest Elements** | ✅ Already minimal; no unnecessary abstractions |\n\n**Other improvements evaluated and rejected:**\n\n1. **Extract `runQuoteStep` / `runClaimStep` helpers** — Rejected: branches are 3–4 statements each; extraction adds mass without clarity gain. The if/else dispatch on a discriminated union is idiomatic and already reads top-to-bottom in story order.\n2. **Switch instead of if/else** — Rejected: pure style change, adds block delimiters, no clarity gain.\n3. **Inline `isFollowup` into options object** — Rejected: the named const labels the boolean; inlining `policies.length > 0` loses that meaning.\n4. **Replace mutation `policies[idx].remainingCap = ...` with spread/Object.assign** — Rejected: mutation is concise and the surrounding comment already explains intent.\n5. **Drop the comment explaining the cap mutation** — Rejected: explains a non-obvious *why* (processClaim returns new cap; we must persist it back so subsequent claims see updated cap).\n\n**Benefits of the rename:**\n- Type, implementation, and tests all now express \"index\" instead of \"policy reference\"\n- A new reader sees `{ op: \"claim\", policyIndex: 0 }` and immediately understands it's a slot into the policies array\n- Doc comment documents the index grows as quote steps occur, removing a \"how does this work?\" question\n\n**Tests**: All 51 passing (8 TODO tests untouched)."}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/minimax-m3","usage":{"input":159,"output":910,"cacheRead":29824,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":401,"totalTokens":30893,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1784957459225,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-de2f1d38-b114-4fbb-9545-b43a4a073ed6","responseModel":"minimax/minimax-m3"}],"stderr":"","usage":{"input":11294,"output":13482,"cacheRead":202752,"cacheWrite":0,"cost":0,"contextTokens":30893,"turns":11},"model":"requesty/tensorx/minimax-m3","stopReason":"stop"}]},"isError":false,"timestamp":1784957474790},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Red -- Test 34: error: unknown item t