seem reasonable. No renaming needed.\n\n## Step 2: Calculate Initial APP Mass\n\n```typescript\nexport function runScenario(scenario: Scenario): { results: StepResult[] } {\n  const results: StepResult[] = [];\n  const policies: Policy[] = [];\n  for (const step of scenario.steps) {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      const isFollowup = policies.length > 0;\n      const premium = quotePremium(step.items, scenario.customer, { isFollowup });\n      policies.push(createPolicy(step.items));\n      results.push({ premium });\n    } else {\n      // processClaim returns the new cap as a value; persist it on the policy\n      // so subsequent claims against the same policy see the updated cap.\n      const result = processClaim(policies[step.policyIndex], step.incident);\n      policies[step.policyIndex].remainingCap = result.remainingCap;\n      results.push(result);\n    }\n  }\n  return { results };\n}\n```\n\nComponents:\n- Constants: `\"quote\"`, `\"claim\"`, `0` (the default), the property `remainingCap` (string literal) - Let's count more carefully\n  - \"quote\" string = 1\n  - \"claim\" string = 1\n  - `0` literal in `policies.length > 0` = 1\n  - `remainingCap` string (property access as key) = 1 (when used as key assignment - debatable)\n  Actually for property assignments, \"remainingCap\" is just a name, not really a constant. Let me skip that.\n  \n  Just consider literal values: \"quote\" = 1, \"claim\" = 1, 0 = 1\n  Total constants: 3\n\n- Bindings:\n  - `results` = 1\n  - `policies` = 1\n  - `step` (loop var) = 1\n  - `isFollowup` = 1\n  - `premium` = 1\n  - `result` = 1\n  Total bindings: 6\n\n- Invocations:\n  - `quotePremium` = 2\n  - `createPolicy` = 2\n  - `processClaim` = 2\n  Total: 6\n\n- Conditionals:\n  - `if (step.op === \"quote\")` = 4\n  - `policies.length > 0` (this is also conditional) = 4\n  Total: 8\n\n- Loops:\n  - `for (const step of scenario.steps)` = 5\n  Total: 5\n\n- Assignments:\n  - `policies.push(...)` - is this an assignment