rpretation: it's a function call (invocation = 2). The mutation is \"built-in\" to the call.\n  \n  I think for simplicity, .push() is an invocation but not an assignment. The .length access is also a property access.\n  \n  Let me just count .push() as an invocation.\n  \n  Actually, the simpler approach: count both .push() and the explicit assignment `policies[step.policyIndex].remainingCap = result.remainingCap;`\n  \n  - `policies.push(...)` - I'll count as invocation (2)\n  - `policies[step.policyIndex].remainingCap = result.remainingCap;` - assignment (6)\n  \n  Total assignments: 6\n\nSo initial mass:\n- Constants: 3\n- Bindings: 6\n- Invocations: 6\n- Conditionals: 8\n- Loops: 5\n- Assignments: 6\n\nTotal: 3 + 6 + 6 + 8 + 5 + 6 = 34\n\nHmm, let me recount more carefully.\n\n```typescript\nexport function runScenario(scenario: Scenario): { results: StepResult[] } {\n  const results: StepResult[] = [];                            // B (results), C (0 from []), A ([] = [])\n  const policies: Policy[] = [];                                // B (policies), C (0 from []), A ([] = [])\n  for (const step of scenario.steps) {                          // L (for...of), B (step)\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {                                  // C (if), C (\"quote\"), C (\"claim\")\n      const isFollowup = policies.length > 0;                   // B (isFollowup), C (0), C (length > 0 cond)\n      const premium = quotePremium(step.items, scenario.customer, { isFollowup }); // B (premium), I (quotePremium), I ({isFollowup} obj)\n      policies.push(createPolicy(step.items));                  // I (createPolicy), I (push)\n      results.push({ premium });                                // I (push)\n    } else {\n      const result = processClaim(policies[step.policyIndex], step.incident);  // B (result), I (processClaim)\n      policies[step.policyIndex].remainingCap = result.remainingCap;           // A (=)\n      results.push(result);                                    