ocessed the step and then pushed to results afterwards.\n\nLet me think about extracting `runQuoteStep` and `runClaimStep` helpers. Would that be clearer?\n\nOption A - current:\n```typescript\nexport function runScenario(scenario: Scenario): { results: StepResult[] } {\n  const results: StepResult[] = [];\n  const policies: Policy[] = [];\n  for (const step of scenario.steps) {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      const isFollowup = policies.length > 0;\n      const premium = quotePremium(step.items, scenario.customer, { isFollowup });\n      policies.push(createPolicy(step.items));\n      results.push({ premium });\n    } else {\n      // processClaim returns the new cap as a value; persist it on the policy\n      // so subsequent claims against the same policy see the updated cap.\n      const result = processClaim(policies[step.policyIndex], step.incident);\n      policies[step.policyIndex].remainingCap = result.remainingCap;\n      results.push(result);\n    }\n  }\n  return { results };\n}\n```\n\nOption B - extract step handlers:\n```typescript\nfunction runQuoteStep(step: QuoteStep, policies: Policy[], customer: Customer): { policy: Policy; result: QuoteResult } {\n  const isFollowup = policies.length > 0;\n  const policy = createPolicy(step.items);\n  const premium = quotePremium(step.items, customer, { isFollowup });\n  return { policy, result: { premium } };\n}\n\nfunction runClaimStep(step: ClaimStep, policies: Policy[]): ClaimResult {\n  const result = processClaim(policies[step.policyIndex], step.incident);\n  policies[step.policyIndex].remainingCap = result.remainingCap;\n  return result;\n}\n\nexport function runScenario(scenario: Scenario): { results: StepResult[] } {\n  const results: StepResult[] = [];\n  const policies: Policy[] = [];\n  for (const step of scenario.steps) {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      const { policy, result } = runQuoteStep(step, policies, scenario.customer);\n      policies.push(policy);\n      results.push(result);\n    } else {\n      const result = runClaimStep(step, policies);\n      results.push(result);\n    }\n  }\n  return { results };\n}\n```\n\nHmm, this adds more code (more elements) and might not really improve clarity. The current code is fine.\n\nLet me think about another angle. The function does several things:\n1. Iterates through steps\n2. Quotes (calculating isFollowup based on previous quotes)\n3. Manages policies array (adding new ones, updating existing)\n4. Records results\n\nThe current code mixes these concerns. Could be cleaner with extract methods, but at the cost of more elements (Rule 4 - Fewest Elements).\n\nActually, looking at the function again, the key insight is that the policies array is mutated in place:\n- `policies.push(createPolicy(step.items))` - adds new policy\n- `policies[step.policyIndex].remainingCap = result.remainingCap` - updates existing\n\nThe comment explains this. But could we avoid this by encapsulating policies in a class/object? That would be over-engineering (Rule 4).\n\nLet me look at this from a different angle. What about the comment? It's pretty verbose. The code is self-explanatory enough. The mutation pattern is common in TypeScript/JavaScript code.\n\nOK let me think about what improvements are truly worth making. The code is pretty clean as is. Let me see if there are minor improvements:\n\n1. The `{ results }` return type could be more meaningful. Currently it's an inline type `{ results: StepResult[] }`. Could be extracted to a named type like `ScenarioResult`. But the current form is concise.\n\nActually wait, looking at this more carefully:\n\n```typescript\nexport type StepResult = QuoteResult | ClaimResult;\n```\n\nThese types already exist. So `{ results: StepResult[] }` is a reasonable shape. Not a big deal.\n\n2. The `// processClaim returns the new cap as a value; persist it on the policy` comment is helpful. I think it's worth keeping.\n\n3. The local variable `result` in the else branch is reused 