\n}\n```\n\nHmm, I notice that in the quote branch, the policy is created AFTER the premium is quoted. But `quotePremium` doesn't depend on the policy (it takes items + customer + options). And `createPolicy` doesn't depend on the premium. So the order is fine but could be swapped.\n\nActually, swapping wouldn't really help. The current order reads well: \"first calculate the premium (which uses isFollowup), then create the policy\".\n\nOK, I'll commit to the small improvement of extracting `policy` in the else branch.\n\nWait, I want to think about this more. Let me see if there are even better improvements.\n\nLooking at the function:\n- It maintains `policies` array as state, mutating it\n- It maintains `results` array, only appending\n\nThe mutation pattern of `policies` could be cleaner. What if we replaced it with an immutable approach? Like:\n\n```typescript\nfunction runScenario(scenario: Scenario): { results: StepResult[] } {\n  const results: StepResult[] = [];\n  const policies: Policy[] = [];\n  for (const step of scenario.steps) {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      const isFollowup = policies.length > 0;\n      const premium = quotePremium(step.items, scenario.customer, { isFollowup });\n      policies.push(createPolicy(step.items));\n      results.push({ premium });\n    } else {\n      const policy = policies[step.policyIndex];\n      const result = processClaim(policy, step.incident);\n      policy.remainingCap = result.remainingCap;\n      results.push(result);\n    }\n  }\n  return { results };\n}\n```\n\nThis is just the small extraction.\n\nAnother consideration: The function takes `scenario` as parameter, but uses `scenario.customer` and `scenario.steps`. Could destructure:\n\n```typescript\nexport function runScenario({ customer, steps }: Scenario): { results: StepResult[] } {\n  const results: StepResult[] = [];\n  const policies: Policy[] = [];\n  for (const step of steps) {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      const isFollowup = policies.length > 0;\n      const premium = quotePremium(step.items, customer, { isFollowup });\n      policies.push(createPolicy(step.items));\n      results.push({ premium });\n    } else {\n      const policy = policies[step.policyIndex];\n      const result = processClaim(policy, step.incident);\n      policy.remainingCap = result.remainingCap;\n      results.push(result);\n    }\n  }\n  return { results };\n}\n```\n\nThis is a minor style change. `customer` and `steps` are now bindings. But `scenario` was a binding before. So binding count is the same.\n\nHmm. Actually this could be considered improvement because we name the parts of the scenario directly. But it's a stylistic preference.\n\nOK, let me make these two small improvements:\n1. Extract `policy` in the else branch\n2. Destructure `scenario` parameter\n\nActually, wait. Looking at the comment:\n\n```typescript\n// processClaim returns the new cap as a value; persist it on the policy\n// so subsequent claims against the same policy see the updated cap.\n```\n\nThis comment is still useful. Let me keep it.\n\nLet me also re-evaluate the file overall. The types are well-organized at the top, then the function. Imports look clean.\n\nOK let me commit to the refactor. I'll:\n1. Extract `policy` in the else branch for clarity (Rule 2)\n2. Destructure `scenario` parameter to avoid `scenario.customer` and `scenario.steps` repetition (Rule 2)\n\nActually, the destructuring is debatable. Let me stick with just the policy extraction for clarity.\n\nActually, looking one more time:\n\n```typescript\n} else {\n  // processClaim returns the new cap as a value; persist it on the policy\n  // so subsequent claims against the same policy see the updated cap.\n  const result = processClaim(policies[step.policyIndex], step.incident);\n  policies[step.policyIndex].remainingCap = result.remainingCap;\n  results.push(result);\n}\n```\n\nThe comment talks about \"processClaim\" and \"the policy\". With `policy` extracted, the code reads:\n1. Get the policy\n2. Process the claim\n3. Update the policy's remaining cap\n4. Add the result\n\nThis is clearer. Let me go with this change.\n\nActually let me think about one more thing. The way `isFollowup` is determined - `policies.length > 0`. This is implicitly \"if we've already done a quote step, this is a follow-up\". But what if the scenario starts with a claim? Then policies is empty, so isFollowup would be false for the first quote. But that's fine because in the test scenarios, quote always comes before claim.\n\nWait, let me re-read the spec. From quote.ts:\n```\nFOLLOWUP_RATE = 0.15;\n```\nAnd: \"Customers receive a 15% discount on each contract after their first.\"\n\nSo `isFollowup` means \"this is a contract after the first\". The first quote step has isFollowup=false, subsequent quote steps have isFollowup=true. This matches the test:\n\n```typescript\nit(\"multi-step: follow-up contract on second quote (isFollowup applied to 2nd quote only)\", () => {\n  // first quote: 100 + 10 first + 5 fee = 115 (no follow-up)\n  expect(result.results[0]).toEqual({ premium: 115 });\n  // second quote: 100 + 10 first - 15 follow-up + 5 fee = 100\n  expect(result.results[1]).toEqual({ premium: 100 });\n});\n```\n\nOK so `policies.length > 0` correctly captures \"have we done a quote before this one?\". \n\nHmm, what about renaming `isFollowup` to something like `isFirstContract` to invert the meaning? No, that would be worse. The current name is fine.\n\nOK, going with the policy extraction improvement.\n\nLet me also verify the comment is still useful after the extraction. Yes, it explains WHY we mutate the policy (so subsequent claims see the updated cap). That context isn't obvious from the code. Keep the comment.\n\nLet me make the change now.\n\nWait, before I commit, let me also consider one more improvement: the `{ results }` return could be extracted to a named type. Looking at quote.ts and policy.ts, they have exported types. Should `ScenarioResult` be an exported type?\n\nCurrently:\n```typescript\nexport function runScenario(scenario: Scenario): { results: StepResult[] } {\n```\n\nIf extracted:\n```typescript\nexport type ScenarioResult = { results: StepResult[] };\n\nexport function runScenario(scenario: Scenario): ScenarioResult {\n```\n\nThis adds 2 lines (type definition + use), which is more elements. But it makes the API more discoverable.\n\nHmm, but is it worth it? The current inline type is concise. The type is only used in one place (the return of runScenario). Rule 4 says \"Keep it simple - don't over-engineer. Only add complexity when it serves clear purpose.\"\n\nI think the inline type is fine. The function name `runScenario` and the inline type tell you exactly what it returns.\n\nOK, final decision: just the policy extraction. Let me do it.\n\nActually wait, I want to reconsider one more time. Let me re-read the instructions:\n\n> **Naming Evaluation (First Refactoring Priority)**:\n>   - Ask: \"Does this name clearly describe what the function actually does based on all tests we have so far?\"\n>   - Ask: \"Has the function's purpose become clearer/more specific through the latest test?\"\n>   - Rename if the name doesn't capture the current full intent\n>   - Especially critical when new functionality changes the nature of what the function does\n\nThe function name `runScenario` is generic. Could it be more specific? The function runs a scenario and returns results. Names:\n- `runScenario` (current)\n- `executeScenario`\n- `processScenario`\n- `playScenario` (suggests gaming scenario)\n- `simulateScenario`\n\n`runScenario` is fine. It's clear and idiomatic. No need to rename.\n\nWhat about types?\n- `QuoteStep` - clear\n- `ClaimStep` - clear\n- `Step` (union) - clear (it's a step in a scenario)\n- `Scenario` - clear\n- `QuoteResult` - clear\n- `ClaimResult` - clear\n- `StepResult` (union) - clear\n\nAll good.\n\nOK going with the policy extraction.\n\nActually, one more consideration. Let me re-look at the code:\n\n```typescript\nfor (const step of scenario.steps) {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      const isFollowup = policies.length > 0;\n      const premium = quotePremium(step.items, scenario.customer, { isFollowup });\n      policies.push(createPolicy(step.items));\n      results.push({ premium });\n    } else {\n      // processClaim returns the new cap as a value; persist it on the policy\n      // so subsequent claims against the same policy see the updated cap.\n      const result = processClaim(policies[step.policyIndex], step.incident);\n      policies[step.policyIndex].remainingCap = result.remainingCap;\n      results.push(result);\n    }\n  }\n```\n\nThe mutation pattern in the else branch is the \"tricky\" part. The function is doing two things to the policies array:\n1. Append new policies (in the quote branch)\n2. Mutate existing policies (in the claim branch)\n\nThese two operations are conceptually different but are unified by the array. This is fine, but maybe slightly less clean than if each policy was encapsulated.\n\nCould we wrap policies in a `PolicyRegistry` class? That would be over-engineering. Rule 4 says don't add unnecessary abstractions.\n\nOK, I'm going to commit to the policy extraction. Let me also reconsider whether to keep the comment.\n\nThe comment is:\n```\n// processClaim returns the new cap as a value; persist it on the policy\n// so subsequent claims against the same policy see the updated cap.\n```\n\nAfter extracting `policy`, the code reads:\n```typescript\n} else {\n  const policy = policies[step.policyIndex];\n  const result = processClaim(policy, step.incident);\n  policy.remainingCap = result.remainingCap;\n  results.push(result);\n}\n```\n\nThe comment is still useful - it explains WHY we update `policy.remainingCap` after the claim. Without the comment, a reader might wonder \"why are we doing this assignment?\". The comment explains it's for subsequent claims.\n\nOK, keep the comment.\n\nLet me now make the change and run tests.\n\nActually, let me reconsider one more potential refactor. What if I extract step handlers?\n\n```typescript\nfunction runQuoteStep(step: QuoteStep, policies: Policy[], customer: Customer): { policy: Policy; result: QuoteResult } {\n  const isFollowup = policies.length > 0;\n  const premium = quotePremium(step.items, customer, { isFollowup });\n  return { policy: createPolicy(step.items), result: { premium } };\n}\n\nfunction runClaimStep(step: ClaimStep, policies: Policy[]): ClaimResult {\n  const policy = policies[step.policyIndex];\n  const result = processClaim(policy, step.incident);\n  policy.remainingCap = result.remainingCap;\n  return result;\n}\n\nexport function runScenario(scenario: Scenario): { results: StepResult[] } {\n  const results: StepResult[] = [];\n  const policies: Policy[] = [];\n  for (const step of scenario.steps) {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      const { policy, result } = runQuoteStep(step, policies, scenario.customer);\n      policies.push(policy);\n      results.push(result);\n    } else {\n      results.push(runClaimStep(step, policies));\n    }\n  }\n  return { results };\n}\n```\n\nHmm, the quote branch still has two operations (push policy, push result). Extracting doesn't fully eliminate that. The claim branch becomes a single push.\n\nTrade-offs:\n- More elements (3 functions vs 1)\n- More mass\n- More focused step handlers (each does one thing)\n- The dispatcher is more focused (just orchestrates)\n\nI think this is borderline. The current code is fine, but extraction could improve clarity. Let me check both versions.\n\nActually, I think the extraction is overkill. The current function is concise and clear. Let me NOT do the extraction and just do the policy extraction.\n\nHmm wait, I want to reconsider once more. Let me look at what makes the code harder to read:\n\n1. The for loop with if/else - this is OK because there are only 2 step types\n2. The mutation of policies array - this is the tricky part\n3. The inline `{ results: StepResult[] }` return type - this is fine\n4. The comment in the else branch - this helps\n\nOK, going with just the policy extraction. Let me do it now.\n\nWait, one more idea: what if we use early returns / continue?\n\n```typescript\nfor (const step of scenario.steps) {\n  if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n    // ...\n    continue;\n  }\n  // claim handling\n}\n```\n\nThis is a common pattern but doesn't really improve clarity here. The if/else is fine.\n\nOK, going with the policy extraction. Let me also double-check if there are any other improvements.\n\nLooking at:\n```typescript\nconst isFollowup = policies.length > 0;\n```\n\nThis is fine. The condition `policies.length > 0` clearly states \"we've done at least one quote before\". The local variable `isFollowup` is named appropriately.\n\nCould the comment in the else branch be moved to the `processClaim` function definition in policy.ts? That might be a better location since it documents the contract of processClaim. But the scenario.ts comment is about how scenario.ts uses processClaim (i.e., it mutates the policy). So it's appropriate here.\n\nActually, looking at policy.ts:\n```typescript\nexport function processClaim(\n  policy: Policy,\n  incident: Incident,\n): { payout: number; remainingCap: number } {\n```\n\nThe return type is `{ payout: number; remainingCap: number }`. The caller needs to persist the new cap. This is a side-effect-free API. The scenario.ts comment documents the caller's responsibility to persist the result.\n\nOK, going with the policy extraction.\n\nLet me also consider: should I refactor the overall structure to be more functional? E.g., using reduce or map?\n\n```typescript\nexport function runScenario(scenario: Scenario): { results: StepResult[] } {\n  const { results, policies } = scenario.steps.reduce(\n    (state, step) => {\n      if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n        // ...\n      } else {\n        // ...\n      }\n      return state;\n    },\n    { results: [], policies: [] }\n  );\n  return { results };\n}\n```\n\nThis is more complex and doesn't really improve clarity. Stick with imperative for/if.\n\nOK, final decision: just extract `policy` in the else branch. Let me do it now.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider one more time.\n\nLooking at the else branch:\n```typescript\nconst result = processClaim(policies[step.policyIndex], step.incident);\npolicies[step.policyIndex].remainingCap = result.remainingCap;\nresults.push(result);\n```\n\nThe pattern is:\n1. Call processClaim with the current policy state\n2. Update the policy with the new cap (to persist for next claim)\n3. Push result\n\nThe mutation is the \"persist\" step. If we had a wrapper like `policy.applyClaim(incident)` that returns the result AND updates the policy internally, the code would be cleaner:\n\n```typescript\n} else {\n  results.push(applyClaim(policies[step.policyIndex], step.incident));\n}\n\nfunction applyClaim(policy: Policy, incident: Incident): ClaimResult {\n  const result = processClaim(policy, incident);\n  policy.remainingCap = result.remainingCap;\n  return result;\n}\n```\n\nThis consolidates the \"process and persist\" pattern. But it adds a new function, which is more elements.\n\nHmm, this is borderline. Let me think...\n\nActually, looking at policy.ts:\n```typescript\nexport function processClaim(\n  policy: Policy,\n  incident: Incident,\n): { payout: number; remainingCap: number } {\n```\n\nThe function name `processClaim` implies it processes a claim and returns the result. It does NOT mutate the policy. This is a pure function. The caller is responsible for persisting the new state.\n\nThis is a reasonable design (separation of pure logic from state mutation). The scenario.ts code correctly uses processClaim and persists the state.\n\nSo the current code is using processClaim correctly. The mutation is the \"persist\" step. Could we add a helper `applyClaim` that does both? It would consolidate the pattern but add an abstraction.\n\nRule 4 says \"Only add complexity when it serves clear purpose.\" If `applyClaim` is used only once, it's just adding an indirection. Not worth it.\n\nOK, going with just the policy extraction.\n\nLet me actually consider if `policy` extraction alone is enough for a meaningful refactoring.\n\nThe change is:\n- 1 line added (`const policy = policies[step.policyIndex];`)\n- 2 lines modified (substitute `policies[step.policyIndex]` with `policy`)\n\nThis is a small, focused improvement. It improves clarity (Rule 2) without breaking tests.\n\nOK, making the change now.\n\nActually, one more thought. The current comment is:\n```\n// processClaim returns the new cap as a value; persist it on the policy\n// so subsequent claims against the same policy see the updated cap.\n```\n\nIf I extract `policy`, the comment can be slightly rephrased:\n```\n// processClaim returns the new cap as a value; persist it on the policy\n// so subsequent claims against the same policy see the updated cap.\n```\n\nThe comment is still accurate. No change needed.\n\nOK, making the change.\n\nLet me also think about: would extracting to `runQuoteStep` and `runClaimStep` be a better refactoring?\n\nPros:\n- Each handler has a single responsibility\n- Easier to test individual step types\n- The dispatcher function is very simple\n\nCons:\n- More elements (more functions)\n- More mass\n- The current code is already clear and concise\n\nHmm. Looking at the codebase style, quote.ts and policy.ts have small helper functions but not extracted step handlers. The style is to keep things in one function if they're focused.\n\nOK, sticking with the policy extraction. Let me do it.\n\nActually, I just realized something. Let me re-read the comment one more time:\n\n```\n// processClaim returns the new cap as a value; persist it on the policy\n// so subsequent claims against the same policy see the updated cap.\n```\n\nThis comment is quite long and explains a specific implementation detail. After extracting `policy`, the code is clearer, but the comment is still useful.\n\nCould I shorten the comment? Maybe:\n```\n// Persist new cap so subsequent claims see the updated cap.\n```\n\nThat's more concise. But the existing comment also explains that processClaim returns the new cap as a value (vs. mutating in place). That's useful context.\n\nHmm. Let me keep the comment as is.\n\nOK, finalizing the refactor:\n\n1. Extract `policy` local variable in the else branch.\n\nLet me also reconsider whether to remove the comment. With `policy` extracted:\n\n```typescript\n} else {\n  const policy = policies[step.policyIndex];\n  const result = processClaim(policy, step.incident);\n  policy.remainingCap = result.remainingCap;\n  results.push(result);\n}\n```\n\nThe mutation `policy.remainingCap = result.remainingCap` could be unclear. Why are we mutating? The comment explains:\n- processClaim returns the new cap\n- We persist it on the policy\n- So subsequent claims see the updated cap\n\nWithout the comment, a reader might think \"why is this assignment here? What is its purpose?\" The comment provides that context.\n\nBut the comment is verbose. Let me see if I can shorten it:\n\n```typescript\n} else {\n  // Persist the updated cap so later claims see it.\n  const policy = policies[step.policyIndex];\n  const result = processClaim(policy, step.incident);\n  policy.remainingCap = result.remainingCap;\n  results.push(result);\n}\n```\n\nThat's shorter and gets to the point. Let me consider keeping it shorter.\n\nActually, the original comment is fine. Let me keep it. Refactoring should be conservative - don't change things that don't need changing.\n\nOK, final plan:\n1. Extract `policy` local variable in the else branch.\n2. Keep the comment as is.\n\nWait, let me also consider this: the destructuring of `scenario` parameter.\n\n```typescript\nexport function runScenario(scenario: Scenario): { results: StepResult[] } {\n```\n\nvs\n\n```typescript\nexport function runScenario({ customer, steps }: Scenario): { results: StepResult[] } {\n```\n\nThe destructured version uses `customer` and `steps` directly without `scenario.` prefix. This:\n- Saves 2 `scenario.` prefixes (minor)\n- Makes the function more focused on its inputs\n- Adds 0 mass (replaces 1 binding with 2)\n\nActually, the mass would be:\n- Before: `scenario` = 1 binding, `scenario.customer` access, `scenario.steps` access = 2 property accesses\n- After: `customer` = 1 binding, `steps` = 1 binding = 2 bindings\n\nProperty accesses aren't counted as bindings or invocations in the APP model (they're free). So:\n- Before: 1 binding\n- After: 2 bindings\n\nMass change: +1\n\nBut the code is slightly cleaner with destructuring. Hmm.\n\nActually, I'm not sure destructuring is better. The `scenario` parameter name provides context. `scenario.customer` clearly says \"the customer from the scenario\". `customer` alone might be ambiguous in a larger context.\n\nLet me NOT do the destructuring. Keep `scenario` as is.\n\nOK, final final plan:\n1. Extract `policy` local variable in the else branch.\n\nLet me make the change.\n\nWait, one more sanity check. Let me re-read the current code:\n\n```typescript\nexport function runScenario(scenario: Scenario): { results: StepResult[] } {\n  const results: StepResult[] = [];\n  const policies: Policy[] = [];\n  for (const step of scenario.steps) {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      const isFollowup = policies.length > 0;\n      const premium = quotePremium(step.items, scenario.customer, { isFollowup });\n      policies.push(createPolicy(step.items));\n      results.push({ premium });\n    } else {\n      // processClaim returns the new cap as a value; persist it on the policy\n      // so subsequent claims against the same policy see the updated cap.\n      const result = processClaim(policies[step.policyIndex], step.incident);\n      policies[step.policyIndex].remainingCap = result.remainingCap;\n      results.push(result);\n    }\n  }\n  return { results };\n}\n```\n\nThe else branch:\n```typescript\nconst result = processClaim(policies[step.policyIndex], step.incident);\npolicies[step.policyIndex].remainingCap = result.remainingCap;\nresults.push(result);\n```\n\nRefactored else branch:\n```typescript\nconst policy = policies[step.policyIndex];\nconst result = processClaim(policy, step.incident);\npolicy.remainingCap = result.remainingCap;\nresults.push(result);\n```\n\nOK, let me make this change.\n\nWait actually, let me also consider: what if I just rearrange the lines to make the mutation clearer?\n\nCurrent:\n```typescript\nconst result = processClaim(policies[step.policyIndex], step.incident);\npolicies[step.policyIndex].remainingCap = result.remainingCap;\nresults.push(result);\n```\n\nRearranged:\n```typescript\nconst policy = policies[step.policyIndex];\nconst result = processClaim(policy, step.incident);\nresults.push(result);\npolicy.remainingCap = result.remainingCap;\n```\n\nThis puts the push before the mutation. But that might be misleading because the push doesn't depend on the mutation.\n\nActually, the current order is fine: process first, persist, then record. The refactored version maintains this order.\n\nOK, making the change now.\n\nLet me also do a final sanity check on the whole refactor.\n\nBefore:\n```typescript\nexport function runScenario(scenario: Scenario): { results: StepResult[] } {\n  const results: StepResult[] = [];\n  const policies: Policy[] = [];\n  for (const step of scenario.steps) {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      const isFollowup = policies.length > 0;\n      const premium = quotePremium(step.items, scenario.customer, { isFollowup });\n      policies.push(createPolicy(step.items));\n      results.push({ premium });\n    } else {\n      // processClaim returns the new cap as a value; persist it on the policy\n      // so subsequent claims against the same policy see the updated cap.\n      const result = processClaim(policies[step.policyIndex], step.incident);\n      policies[step.policyIndex].remainingCap = result.remainingCap;\n      results.push(result);\n    }\n  }\n  return { results };\n}\n```\n\nAfter:\n```typescript\nexport function runScenario(scenario: Scenario): { results: StepResult[] } {\n  const results: StepResult[] = [];\n  const policies: Policy[] = [];\n  for (const step of scenario.steps) {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      const isFollowup = policies.length > 0;\n      const premium = quotePremium(step.items, scenario.customer, { isFollowup });\n      policies.push(createPolicy(step.items));\n      results.push({ premium });\n    } else {\n      // processClaim returns the new cap as a value; persist it on the policy\n      // so subsequent claims against the same policy see the updated cap.\n      const policy = policies[step.policyIndex];\n      const result = processClaim(policy, step.incident);\n      policy.remainingCap = result.remainingCap;\n      results.push(result);\n    }\n  }\n  return { results };\n}\n```\n\nThis is a single, focused improvement: extract `policy` to name the entity being mutated. It improves clarity (Rule 2) without adding significant mass.\n\nLet me make the change and run tests.\n\nActually, wait. I want to reconsider one more time whether there's a more meaningful refactoring.\n\nLooking at the overall file structure:\n- Imports (2 lines)\n- Types (QuoteStep, ClaimStep, Step, Scenario, QuoteResult, ClaimResult, StepResult) - 7 types\n- Function (runScenario) - 18 lines\n\nThe types are well-organized. Could be moved to a separate types file, but that's over-engineering for this size.\n\nThe function is concise and clear. The only real improvement I see is the policy extraction.\n\nOK, committing to this refactor. Let me make the change.\n\nHmm, one more thing. Let me look at whether the import statements are minimal:\n\n```typescript\nimport { createPolicy, processClaim, type Policy, type Incident } from \"./policy.js\";\nimport { quotePremium, type QuoteItem, type Customer } from \"./quote.js\";\n```\n\nUsed:\n- createPolicy: yes (in quote branch)\n- processClaim: yes (in else branch)\n- type Policy: yes (in `policies: Policy[]` and `policyIndex` types indirectly via Policy[])\n- type Incident: yes (in ClaimStep type)\n- quotePremium: yes (in quote branch)\n- type QuoteItem: yes (in QuoteStep type)\n- type Customer: yes (in Scenario type)\n\nAll imports are used. No cleanup needed.\n\nOK, making the change now.\n\nWait, let me also check eslint config and see if there are any rules I'm missing.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"I'll refactor the scenario.ts implementation. Let me first check the eslint config and look at the current state more thoroughly."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_74d0d680155746918e042df5","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_02-01-15_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2.1-phase-continuation-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/eslint.config.mjs"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/minimax-m3","usage":{"input":367,"output":12072,"cacheRead":15232,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":11973,"totalTokens":27671,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784957546290,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-29e36266-8212-41d1-bee6-572c9dd5ca82","responseModel":"minimax/minimax-m3"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_74d0d680155746918e042df5","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"import sonarjs from \"eslint-plugin-sonarjs\";\nimport tseslint from \"typescript-eslint\";\n\nexport default [\n  {\n    files: [\"src/**/*.ts\"],\n    ignores: [\"src/**/*.spec.ts\"],\n    languageOptions: {\n      parser: tseslint.parser,\n      parserOptions: {\n        projectService: true,\n      },\n    },\n    plugins: {\n      sonarjs,\n    },\n    rules: {\n      // Complexity smells\n      \"sonarj