at I have. It removes the temporary `input` binding. Cleaner.\n\nOK let me apply the refactoring.\n\nActually wait, I want to also consider whether `main` could be simplified further. Currently:\n\n```typescript\nasync function main(): Promise<void> {\n  try {\n    const scenario = JSON.parse(await readStdin());\n    process.stdout.write(JSON.stringify(runScenario(scenario)));\n  } catch (err) {\n    reportErrorAndExit(err);\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThat's pretty clean. The `try` wraps both parsing and running because both can throw, and the catch handles them uniformly. Good.\n\nOne more thought: could we avoid `process.exit(1)` and instead let the unhandled promise rejection handle it? Probably not, because we want to write to stderr first.\n\nOK, let me also consider: do we even need to wrap `main()` in a try/catch if `reportErrorAndExit` calls `process.exit(1)`?\n\nIf `reportErrorAndExit` is called, it never returns (return type `never`), so the function exits. The try/catch is what catches the errors from `JSON.parse` and `runScenario`. Yes, the try/catch is needed.\n\nAlright, let me apply the refactoring.\n\nActually, one more consideration: the `process.stdin.setEncoding(\"utf8\")` - should this be in `readStdin` or be implicit? Since we're reading a stream and want text, setting utf8 encoding is appropriate. Keep it.\n\nLet me write the final refactored version:\n\n```typescript\nimport { runScenario } from \"./scenario.js\";\n\n/** Read all of stdin as a UTF-8 string. */\nasync function readStdin(): Promise<string> {\n  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\n    let data = \"\";\n    process.stdin.setEncoding(\"utf8\");\n    process.stdin.on(\"data\", (chunk) => { data += chunk; });\n    process.stdin.on(\"end\", () => resolve(data));\n    process.stdin.on(\"error\", reject);\n  });\n}\n\n/** Write `err`'s message to stderr and exit the process with code 1. */\nfunction reportErrorAndExit(err: unknown): never {\n  const message = err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err);\n  process.stderr.write(message + \"\\n\");\n  process.exit(1);\n}\n\nasync function main(): Promise<void> {\n  try {\n    const scenario = JSON.parse(await readStdin());\n    process.stdout.write(JSON.stringify(runScenario(scenario)));\n  } catch (err) {\n    reportErrorAndExit(err);\n  }\n}\n\nmain();\n```\n\nLet me apply this and run the tests.\n\nWait, before I do, let me also consider:\n\nThe `process.exit(1)` returns `never` to TypeScript. Good. I've used `never` as the return type.\n\nActually one small thing - the variable shadowing concern. In `main`, `err` is caught. In `reportErrorAndExit`, `err` is the parameter. Both named `err`. That's fine, they're in different scopes.\n\nOK let me apply the refactoring.\n\nOh wait, one more thing. The tests pass stdin as a string and end it. Let me trace through:\n1. Test spawns `npx tsx src/cli.ts`\n2. Test sends JSON via stdin\n3. Test closes stdin (via `proc.stdin.end(stdin)`)\n4. CLI's `readStdin` reads all data and resolves\n5. CLI parses JSON, runs scenario, writes JSON to stdout\n6. CLI exits\n\nThat's the happy path. The tests pass.\n\nLet me now apply the refactoring.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Now let me run the tests to confirm baseline, then analyze and refactor."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_54d0debfe2644554b9dadc16","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_02-01-15_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2.1-phase-continuation-pi_minimax-m3-no-thinking/ && pnpm test 2>&1 | tail -10"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/minimax-m3","usage":{"input":260,"output":6386,"cacheRead":12544,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":6285,"totalTokens":19190,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784957867494,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-f3628e6e-19ae-4a0b-8011-e855596669ae","responseModel":"minimax/minimax-m3"},{"role":"toolResult","tool